window.HSCI208_DATA={"course":"HSci 208","title":"The Integumentary, Musculoskeletal, and Immune Systems","sets":[{"id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","title":"Doc Ade — Integ Quiz 1","subtitle":"Embryology & Physiology","source":"integumentary-quiz-1.docx","key_status":"not_provided","questions":[{"source_number":"1","prompt":"The dermis of the trunk and limbs differentiates from which embryonic structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neural crest"},{"label":"B","text":"Dermatome of somites"},{"label":"C","text":"Surface ectoderm"},{"label":"D","text":"Endoderm"}],"type":"mcq","answer":null,"id":"integ-quiz-1-1","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"pending","rationale":"This item is region-dependent: dorsal trunk dermis derives from dermatome, while ventrolateral body wall and limb dermis derive largely from lateral plate mesoderm. Because the stem groups 'trunk and limbs' together and lateral plate mesoderm is not offered, it is best left for instructor confirmation."},{"source_number":"2","prompt":"Which skin appendages share a common surface ectoderm origin with the epidermis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nails and sweat glands only"},{"label":"B","text":"Dermal papillae and blood vessels"},{"label":"C","text":"Hair follicles and sebaceous glands only"},{"label":"D","text":"Only hair follicles"},{"label":"E","text":"Hair, nails, sebaceous glands, sweat glands, and mammary glands"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"E","id":"integ-quiz-1-2","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Hair, nails, sebaceous glands, sweat glands, and mammary glands are epidermal appendages derived from surface ectoderm."},{"source_number":"3","prompt":"The hypodermis (subcutaneous tissue) is derived from which germ layer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endoderm"},{"label":"B","text":"Surface ectoderm"},{"label":"C","text":"Neural crest"},{"label":"D","text":"Mesoderm"},{"label":"E","text":"Ectoderm"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"integ-quiz-1-3","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"The hypodermis is connective tissue/adipose tissue and is mesodermal in origin."},{"source_number":"4","prompt":"The arrector pili muscle, which attaches to hair follicles and causes piloerection, is derived from which embryonic tissue?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neural crest"},{"label":"B","text":"Mesoderm"},{"label":"C","text":"Endoderm"},{"label":"D","text":"Periderm"},{"label":"E","text":"Surface ectoderm"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"integ-quiz-1-4","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Arrector pili is smooth muscle; smooth muscle connective-tissue derivatives are mesodermal."},{"source_number":"5","prompt":"What is the first visible change in epidermal development during the 4th week of gestation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Formation of dermal papillae"},{"label":"B","text":"Single-layer surface ectoderm covering the embryo"},{"label":"C","text":"Formation of stratum corneum"},{"label":"D","text":"Appearance of melanocytes"},{"label":"E","text":"Development of rete ridges"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"integ-quiz-1-5","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Early skin is initially covered by a single layer of surface ectoderm before epidermal stratification begins."},{"source_number":"6","prompt":"The bilaminar stage of epidermal development (weeks 4-5) is characterized by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Development of hair follicles"},{"label":"B","text":"Migration of melanocytes into the epidermis"},{"label":"C","text":"Formation of all five epidermal layers"},{"label":"D","text":"Division of ectoderm into periderm and stratum germinativum"},{"label":"E","text":"Appearance of stratum corneum"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"integ-quiz-1-6","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"The early bilaminar epidermis consists of a superficial periderm and a basal stratum germinativum."},{"source_number":"7","prompt":"By what gestational age does the epidermis acquire its definitive 4-5 layered histologic strata?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"8-10 weeks"},{"label":"B","text":"24-28 weeks"},{"label":"C","text":"11-13 weeks"},{"label":"D","text":"32-36 weeks"},{"label":"E","text":"16-20 weeks (4th-5th month)"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"E","id":"integ-quiz-1-7","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"By roughly the 4th–5th fetal month, the epidermis has differentiated into its recognizable definitive strata."},{"source_number":"8","prompt":"The transient periderm, which serves as a protective cover during early epidermal development, is eventually:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Differentiated into hair follicles"},{"label":"B","text":"Incorporated into the stratum basale"},{"label":"C","text":"Desquamated and contributes to vernix caseosa"},{"label":"D","text":"Absorbed into the dermis"},{"label":"E","text":"Transformed into melanocytes"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"integ-quiz-1-8","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Periderm cells are shed into the amniotic fluid and contribute, together with sebum, to vernix caseosa."},{"source_number":"9","prompt":"The vernix caseosa is composed primarily of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amniotic fluid proteins"},{"label":"B","text":"Keratin and collagen"},{"label":"C","text":"Fetal urine and sweat"},{"label":"D","text":"Desquamated periderm cells and sebum"},{"label":"E","text":"Maternal hormones and antibodies"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"integ-quiz-1-9","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Vernix is mainly desquamated peridermal cells mixed with lipid-rich sebaceous secretion."},{"source_number":"10","prompt":"The epidermis is avascular and receives nutrients and oxygen through:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Its own vascular network"},{"label":"B","text":"Diffusion from the underlying dermis"},{"label":"C","text":"Direct absorption from amniotic fluid"},{"label":"D","text":"Transplacental transfer only"},{"label":"E","text":"Lymphatic vessels within the epidermis"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"integ-quiz-1-10","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"The epidermis is avascular, so oxygen and nutrients diffuse from capillaries in the underlying dermis."},{"source_number":"11","prompt":"Newborns with reduced vernix caseosa at birth are at increased risk for:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cutaneous barrier impairment and fluid loss"},{"label":"B","text":"Neonatal jaundice"},{"label":"C","text":"Excessive weight gain"},{"label":"D","text":"Respiratory distress syndrome"},{"label":"E","text":"Hyperbilirubinemia"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"integ-quiz-1-11","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Loss of vernix reduces surface barrier protection and increases transepidermal fluid/heat loss."},{"source_number":"12","prompt":"Which neonatal population is at highest risk for absence of vernix caseosa and its associated complications?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infants born at 38-40 weeks gestation"},{"label":"B","text":"Term infants with adequate birth weight"},{"label":"C","text":"Infants born via cesarean section"},{"label":"D","text":"Infants with polyhydramnios"},{"label":"E","text":"Post-term and fetal growth-restricted (FGR) infants"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"E","id":"integ-quiz-1-12","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Post-term infants often have reduced vernix, and growth-restricted infants may also have impaired protective skin/subcutaneous reserves."},{"source_number":"13","prompt":"A neonate born at 32 weeks gestation is noted to have translucent, gelatinous skin with minimal vernix caseosa. Which of the following best explains this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The infant has a skin infection"},{"label":"B","text":"The infant is post-term"},{"label":"C","text":"The mother had gestational diabetes"},{"label":"D","text":"There is a genetic disorder of keratinization"},{"label":"E","text":"Vernix formation is incomplete at this gestational age"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"E","id":"integ-quiz-1-13","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"At 32 weeks, epidermal/vernix maturation is still incomplete compared with term skin, explaining a more translucent barrier."},{"source_number":"14","prompt":"Melanoblasts originate from the neural crest and first arrive in the developing epidermis by which gestational week?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4th week"},{"label":"B","text":"8th week"},{"label":"C","text":"24th week"},{"label":"D","text":"11th week"},{"label":"E","text":"16th week"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"integ-quiz-1-14","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Neural crest-derived melanoblasts migrate dorsolaterally and reach the developing epidermis at about the 8th week."},{"source_number":"15","prompt":"Mature melanocytes in the epidermis are located primarily in which layer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stratum spinosum"},{"label":"B","text":"Stratum basale"},{"label":"C","text":"Stratum corneum"},{"label":"D","text":"Stratum lucidum"},{"label":"E","text":"Stratum granulosum"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"integ-quiz-1-15","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Mature melanocytes reside primarily in the stratum basale and transfer melanin to neighboring keratinocytes."},{"source_number":"16","prompt":"Oculocutaneous albinism is characterized by which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperproliferation of melanoblasts"},{"label":"B","text":"Normal melanocyte migration with impaired melanin synthesis"},{"label":"C","text":"Absence of melanocytes in the skin"},{"label":"D","text":"Defective melanoblast migration from neural crest"},{"label":"E","text":"Excessive melanin production"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"integ-quiz-1-16","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"In oculocutaneous albinism, melanocytes are generally present but melanin synthesis is impaired."},{"source_number":"17","prompt":"Waardenburg syndrome combines pigmentary abnormalities with:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Visual impairment"},{"label":"B","text":"Renal dysfunction"},{"label":"C","text":"Sensorineural hearing loss"},{"label":"D","text":"Cardiac defects"},{"label":"E","text":"Skeletal abnormalities"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"integ-quiz-1-17","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Waardenburg syndrome is a neural crest disorder classically combining pigmentary changes with sensorineural hearing loss."},{"source_number":"18","prompt":"Aplasia cutis congenita most commonly presents as:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Widespread blistering of the skin"},{"label":"B","text":"Localized absence of skin on the scalp vertex"},{"label":"C","text":"Thickened, scaly skin"},{"label":"D","text":"Depigmented patches on the trunk"},{"label":"E","text":"Excessive hair growth"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"integ-quiz-1-18","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Aplasia cutis congenita most often appears as a localized congenital absence of skin on the scalp vertex."},{"source_number":"19","prompt":"Cutis marmorata in neonates is characterized by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blister formation"},{"label":"B","text":"Absence of the epidermis"},{"label":"C","text":"A reticulated mottled vascular pattern"},{"label":"D","text":"Excessive hair growth"},{"label":"E","text":"Thickened, hyperpigmented skin"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"integ-quiz-1-19","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Cutis marmorata is the transient reticulated/mottled vascular pattern seen especially with cold exposure in neonates."},{"source_number":"20","prompt":"Lanugo hair typically emerges at the skin surface by which gestational week?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"16th week"},{"label":"B","text":"12th week"},{"label":"C","text":"32nd week"},{"label":"D","text":"28th week"},{"label":"E","text":"20th week"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"E","id":"integ-quiz-1-20","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Lanugo becomes conspicuous around midgestation, approximately the 20th week."},{"source_number":"21","prompt":"Nail development initiates around the 10th week as:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Keratinized nail cells appear"},{"label":"B","text":"Complete nail plates form on all digits"},{"label":"C","text":"Blood vessels invade the nail bed"},{"label":"D","text":"Localized epidermal thickening on the tips of the digits"},{"label":"E","text":"Dermal papillae form beneath the nails"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"integ-quiz-1-21","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Nails begin as localized epidermal thickenings (nail fields) at the distal digits before formation of the nail plate."},{"source_number":"22","prompt":"Which statement accurately describes the functional status of eccrine sweat glands in newborns?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They are functional at birth but structurally immature"},{"label":"B","text":"They are only functional on the palms and soles"},{"label":"C","text":"They are fully mature and functional at birth"},{"label":"D","text":"They do not develop until the neonatal period"},{"label":"E","text":"They are nonfunctional until puberty"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"integ-quiz-1-22","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Eccrine glands are present and can function at birth, although neonatal sweating/thermoregulation remains immature."},{"source_number":"23","prompt":"Apocrine sweat glands remain completely inactive until:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infancy"},{"label":"B","text":"Puberty"},{"label":"C","text":"Childhood"},{"label":"D","text":"Birth"},{"label":"E","text":"Adulthood"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"integ-quiz-1-23","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Apocrine glands become functionally active at puberty under hormonal influence."},{"source_number":"24","prompt":"Mammary glands develop from which embryonic structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neural crest cells"},{"label":"B","text":"The dermatome"},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral plate mesoderm"},{"label":"D","text":"Endodermal buds"},{"label":"E","text":"The mammary ridge (milk line)"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"E","id":"integ-quiz-1-24","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Mammary glands arise along the ectodermal mammary ridge or milk line."},{"source_number":"25","prompt":"Hypohidrotic ectodermal dysplasia results from mutations affecting which signaling pathway?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sonic hedgehog signaling"},{"label":"B","text":"Notch signaling"},{"label":"C","text":"TGF-beta signaling"},{"label":"D","text":"EDA/EDAR (ectodysplasin) signaling"},{"label":"E","text":"Wnt signaling"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"integ-quiz-1-25","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Hypohidrotic ectodermal dysplasia commonly involves EDA–EDAR signaling, which regulates ectodermal appendage development."},{"source_number":"26","prompt":"The clinical tetrad of hypohidrotic ectodermal dysplasia includes:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sparse hair, peg teeth, nail fragility, and hyperhidrosis"},{"label":"B","text":"Hypotrichosis, hypodontia, nail dystrophies, and anhidrosis"},{"label":"C","text":"Normal hair, dental anomalies, nail dystrophies, and anhidrosis"},{"label":"D","text":"Hypertrichosis, hypodontia, nail hypertrophy, and hyperhidrosis"},{"label":"E","text":"Alopecia, dental caries, nail fragility, and sweating"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"integ-quiz-1-26","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"The classic phenotype includes hypotrichosis, hypodontia/abnormal teeth, nail abnormalities, and hypohidrosis/anhidrosis."},{"source_number":"27","prompt":"Epidermolysis bullosa is caused by mutations in genes encoding:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Structural proteins that anchor the epidermis to the dermis"},{"label":"B","text":"Ectodysplasin signaling proteins"},{"label":"C","text":"Collagen synthesis enzymes"},{"label":"D","text":"Enzymes for melanin synthesis"},{"label":"E","text":"Keratinization proteins"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"integ-quiz-1-27","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Epidermolysis bullosa results from defects in structural proteins that maintain adhesion between epidermis, basement membrane, and dermis."},{"source_number":"28","prompt":"Congenital ichthyosis represents a family of disorders characterized by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Absence of melanin synthesis"},{"label":"B","text":"Blister formation after minor trauma"},{"label":"C","text":"Profound keratinization or lipid processing defects"},{"label":"D","text":"Defective collagen synthesis"},{"label":"E","text":"Failure of neural crest migration"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"integ-quiz-1-28","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Congenital ichthyoses are disorders of cornification caused by defects in keratinization and/or epidermal lipid processing."},{"source_number":"29","prompt":"The stratum corneum provides barrier protection primarily through:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tight junctions between living keratinocytes"},{"label":"B","text":"Melanin granules"},{"label":"C","text":"Antimicrobial peptides"},{"label":"D","text":"Desmosomal connections"},{"label":"E","text":"Lipid-rich, enucleated cornified cells"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"E","id":"integ-quiz-1-29","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"The stratum corneum barrier is formed by anucleate cornified cells embedded in extracellular lipids."},{"source_number":"30","prompt":"Merkel cells in the epidermis function as:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pigment-producing cells"},{"label":"B","text":"Antigen-presenting cells"},{"label":"C","text":"Mechanoreceptors for light touch"},{"label":"D","text":"Thermoregulatory cells"},{"label":"E","text":"Keratin-producing cells"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"integ-quiz-1-30","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Merkel cells are slowly adapting mechanoreceptor-associated cells involved in light touch/pressure sensation."},{"source_number":"31","prompt":"Cutaneous vasodilation in response to heat is mediated by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased sympathetic noradrenergic tone"},{"label":"B","text":"Increased parasympathetic activity"},{"label":"C","text":"Direct effects of acetylcholine"},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased sympathetic tone to arteriovenous anastomoses"},{"label":"E","text":"Local cooling reflexes"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"integ-quiz-1-31","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"In arteriovenous anastomoses, heat-induced vasodilation can result from withdrawal of sympathetic vasoconstrictor tone."},{"source_number":"32","prompt":"The neural pathway controlling eccrine sweat gland secretion involves:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autonomic adrenergic fibers"},{"label":"B","text":"Parasympathetic cholinergic fibers"},{"label":"C","text":"Somatic motor nerves"},{"label":"D","text":"Sympathetic noradrenergic fibers"},{"label":"E","text":"Sympathetic cholinergic fibers"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"E","id":"integ-quiz-1-32","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Eccrine sweating is unusual autonomically because it is driven by sympathetic postganglionic cholinergic fibers."},{"source_number":"33","prompt":"The first step in vitamin D synthesis occurs when:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cholecalciferol is transported in the blood"},{"label":"B","text":"7-dehydrocholesterol is converted to previtamin D3 in the kidney"},{"label":"C","text":"25-hydroxyvitamin D is converted in the kidney"},{"label":"D","text":"Vitamin D3 is hydroxylated in the liver"},{"label":"E","text":"7-dehydrocholesterol in keratinocytes is photolyzed by UVB radiation"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"E","id":"integ-quiz-1-33","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"UVB photolyzes 7-dehydrocholesterol in epidermal keratinocytes to previtamin D3, the first step in cutaneous vitamin D synthesis."},{"source_number":"34","prompt":"Hypothermia in a preterm infant results from all of the following EXCEPT:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High surface-area-to-volume ratio"},{"label":"B","text":"Thin stratum corneum"},{"label":"C","text":"Reduced vernix caseosa"},{"label":"D","text":"Immature thermoregulatory mechanisms"},{"label":"E","text":"Abundant subcutaneous fat"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"E","id":"integ-quiz-1-34","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Abundant subcutaneous fat would reduce heat loss; preterm infants instead have little insulating fat plus immature barrier/thermoregulation."},{"source_number":"35","prompt":"During the inflammation phase of wound healing (days 1-4), the primary cellular functions include:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phagocytosis of pathogens and necrotic debris"},{"label":"B","text":"Vasoconstriction and platelet activation"},{"label":"C","text":"Keratinocyte migration and epithelialization"},{"label":"D","text":"Keratinocyte migration and epithelialization"},{"label":"E","text":"Collagen synthesis and angiogenesis"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"integ-quiz-1-35","set_id":"integ-quiz-1","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"The inflammatory phase is dominated by neutrophils/macrophages that remove microbes and necrotic debris; epithelial migration and collagen deposition dominate later phases."}]},{"id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","title":"Doc Ade — Integ Quiz 2","subtitle":"Clinical Integumentary Concepts","source":"integumentary-quiz-2.docx","key_status":"not_provided","questions":[{"source_number":"1","prompt":"A newborn with Junctional Epidermolysis Bullosa (JEB) has a defect in the basement membrane zone. During embryonic development, which of the following correctly pairs the primary germ layer origin of the epidermis with the origin of the dermis in the limbs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ectoderm; Neural Crest Cells"},{"label":"B","text":"Endoderm; Paraxial Mesoderm"},{"label":"C","text":"Mesoderm; Surface Ectoderm"},{"label":"D","text":"Ectoderm; Lateral Plate Mesoderm"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"integ-quiz-2-1","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"The epidermis derives from surface ectoderm, while dermis of the limbs is derived largely from lateral plate mesoderm."},{"source_number":"2","prompt":"A skin biopsy from a patient with a suspected form of Epidermolysis Bullosa shows a cleavage plane below the lamina densa. Immunofluorescence staining would most likely reveal an absence of which protein?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Laminin-332"},{"label":"B","text":"Plectin"},{"label":"C","text":"Collagen XVII"},{"label":"D","text":"IIntegrin α6β4"},{"label":"E","text":"Type VII Collagen"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"E","id":"integ-quiz-2-2","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa splits below the lamina densa because type VII collagen anchoring fibrils are defective."},{"source_number":"3","prompt":"A child with Hypohidrotic Ectodermal Dysplasia (HED) develops a dangerously high fever during a mild playdate on a warm day. The primary physiological mechanism for this hyperthermia is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Excessive heat production from shivering due to the child's inability to sweat."},{"label":"B","text":"Impaired hypothalamic regulation of body temperature due to a neural crest defect."},{"label":"C","text":"Failure of radiation and convection to dissipate heat in a warm environment, combined with the absence of evaporative cooling."},{"label":"D","text":"Increased metabolic heat production due to the underlying genetic defect."},{"label":"E","text":"Increased skin blood flow that paradoxically traps heat in the core."}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"integ-quiz-2-3","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"In a warm environment radiation/convection become less effective, so loss of eccrine evaporative cooling can cause dangerous hyperthermia."},{"source_number":"4","prompt":"A 60-year-old patient and a 25-year-old patient sustain identical, clean surgical incisions. The older patient's wound heals significantly slower. Which of the following is the primary age-related change in the skin that contributes to this delayed healing?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Overproduction of matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs) that degrade the newly formed clot."},{"label":"B","text":"Increased cellular turnover in the epidermis leading to a thicker stratum corneum."},{"label":"C","text":"Increased production of collagen and elastin, making the tissue more rigid."},{"label":"D","text":"Thinner skin, reduced vascularity, and a blunted inflammatory response."},{"label":"E","text":"Enhanced inflammatory response leading to excessive scar tissue formation."}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"integ-quiz-2-4","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Aging skin is thinner and less vascular, with reduced inflammatory/proliferative responses, all of which slow wound repair."},{"source_number":"5","prompt":"Vernix caseosa serves multiple protective functions for the developing fetus. Which of the following correctly pairs a component of vernix with its corresponding physiologic function?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amniotic fluid proteins — Promotion of non-shivering thermogenesis"},{"label":"B","text":"Desquamated periderm cells — Production of antimicrobial peptides"},{"label":"C","text":"Melanocytes — Protection against intrauterine UV radiation"},{"label":"D","text":"Sebum — Formation of a waterproof barrier and prevention of tissue maceration"},{"label":"E","text":"Lanugo hair — Facilitation of rapid skin acidification at birth"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"integ-quiz-2-5","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Sebum supplies surface lipids that waterproof fetal skin and help protect it from prolonged maceration in amniotic fluid."},{"source_number":"6","prompt":"In oculocutaneous albinism type 1 (OCA1A), the skin appears white-pink. This is primarily because:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The melanocytes have been destroyed by an autoimmune process."},{"label":"B","text":"The number of melanocytes in the epidermis is significantly reduced."},{"label":"C","text":"The melanosomes are trapped in the keratinocytes and cannot be degraded."},{"label":"D","text":"The melanocytes are present but are unable to produce melanin due to a defective enzyme."},{"label":"E","text":"The migration of melanoblasts to the epidermis was completely blocked."}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"integ-quiz-2-6","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"OCA1A is classically caused by absent tyrosinase activity: melanocytes are present but cannot synthesize melanin."},{"source_number":"7","prompt":"A 45-year-old adult sustains third-degree burns over the entire anterior and posterior surfaces of both legs. According to the Rule of Nines, what is the approximate total body surface area (TBSA) affected, and what is the most immediate life-threatening complication? : oe )","options":[{"label":"A","text":"45%; Skin infection"},{"label":"B","text":"18%; Dehydration and hypovolaemia"},{"label":"C","text":"36%; Hypothermia"},{"label":"D","text":"54%; Renal failure"},{"label":"E","text":"36%; Dehydration and hypovolaemia"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"E","id":"integ-quiz-2-7","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Each entire leg is about 18% TBSA in an adult, so both legs are ~36%; early extensive burns threaten intravascular volume through fluid loss/capillary leak."},{"source_number":"8","prompt":"An infant presents with congenital nystagmus, pale blue irises, and white hair. Genetic testing reveals a mutation in the TYR gene. Which of the following best explains the embryologic basis for the nystagmus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The sclera is abnormally thin, causing the eye to be misshapen and leading to unstable fixation."},{"label":"B","text":"The pupillary muscles are hypoplastic due to a lack of neural crest-derived cells."},{"label":"C","text":"The absence of melanin in the developing eye disrupts the normal routing of retinal ganglion cell axons at the optic v chiasm."},{"label":"D","text":"The lack of melanin in the cornea leads to abnormal light refraction and optic nerve damage."},{"label":"E","text":"The neural crest cells that form the optic nerve fail to migrate properly."}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"integ-quiz-2-8","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Reduced ocular melanin during development alters optic-pathway organization, including misrouting of retinal ganglion cell axons at the optic chiasm."},{"source_number":"9","prompt":"The basement membrane zone (BMZ) is a complex structure. A defect in which of the following proteins would most directly disrupt the intracellular connection between the keratin intermediate filaments and the hemidesmosomal plaque?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type VII Collagen"},{"label":"B","text":"Integrin a6p4"},{"label":"C","text":"Laminin-332"},{"label":"D","text":"PlectinY"},{"label":"E","text":"Type lV Collagen"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"integ-quiz-2-9","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Plectin links keratin intermediate filaments to the intracellular hemidesmosomal plaque."},{"source_number":"10","prompt":"A key embryologic distinction explains why albinism affects the eye. The retinal pigment epithelium (RPE) and the melanocytes of the iris have different developmental origins. The RPE is derived from the ____, while the iris melanocytes are derived from the ____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Surface ectoderm; Neural crest"},{"label":"B","text":"Neuroectoderm; Neural crest"},{"label":"C","text":"Neural crest; Neuroectoderm"},{"label":"D","text":"Neural crest; Mesoderm"},{"label":"E","text":"Mesoderm; Neuroectoderm"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"integ-quiz-2-10","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Retinal pigment epithelium is neuroectodermal, while stromal melanocytes of the iris are neural crest derived."},{"source_number":"11","prompt":"A family history reveals that a patient's maternal uncle is also affected by HED. The patient's mother is an asymptomatic carrier. This inheritance pattern is characteristic of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autosomal dominant inheritance."},{"label":"B","text":"X-linked dominant inheritance."},{"label":"C","text":"Mitochondrial inheritance"},{"label":"D","text":"Autosomal recessive inheritance."},{"label":"E","text":"X-linked recessive inheritance."}],"type":"mcq","answer":"E","id":"integ-quiz-2-11","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"The pedigree described—affected males connected through carrier females—is characteristic of X-linked recessive inheritance."},{"source_number":"12","prompt":"A patient sustains a deep, contaminated wound. The wound is left open to heal by secondary intention. Which of the following complications is of greatest concern during the inflammatory phase of this process?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The development of a contracture, leading to functional impairment"},{"label":"B","text":"The formation of a fine, nearly invisible scar"},{"label":"C","text":"The rapid proliferation of epithelial cells, leading to inclusion cysts"},{"label":"D","text":"The establishment of a localized infection due to the presence of contamination and devitalized tissueY"},{"label":"E","text":"Excessive granulation tissue that delays epithelialization"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"integ-quiz-2-12","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"A contaminated open wound is especially vulnerable to infection during the inflammatory phase because devitalized tissue and microbes remain present."},{"source_number":"13","prompt":"Senescent cells in the skin secrete a Senescence-Associated Secretory Phenotype (SASP). Which of the following is a direct consequence of the SASP that perpetuates the aging process?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It inhibits the activity of matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs), preserving the extracellular matrix"},{"label":"B","text":"It induces a chronic pro-inflammatory state (\"inflammaging\") and can cause paracrine senescence in neighboring cells"},{"label":"C","text":"It increases the skin's antioxidant capacity, protecting against future UV damage"},{"label":"D","text":"It directly stimulates the synthesis of type | procollagen"},{"label":"E","text":"It promotes angiogenesis and improves wound healing capacity"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"integ-quiz-2-13","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"The SASP promotes chronic low-grade inflammation and can drive paracrine senescence in neighboring cells."},{"source_number":"14","prompt":"During embryonic development, the periderm plays a critical role in early skin formation. Which of the following statements best describes the fate and function of the periderm?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The periderm is a temporary surface layer that sloughs off and contributes to the formation of vernix caseosa"},{"label":"B","text":"The periderm persists throughout life as the outermost layer of the epidermis"},{"label":"C","text":"The periderm becomes the stratum corneum after functional keratinization is complete"},{"label":"D","text":"The periderm differentiates into sebaceous glands and hair follicles"},{"label":"E","text":"The periderm is derived from the mesoderm and provides structural support to the developing epidermis"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"integ-quiz-2-14","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"The periderm is a temporary fetal surface layer that is later shed and contributes to vernix caseosa."},{"source_number":"15","prompt":"A 3-year-old boy presents with sparse hair, peg-shaped teeth, and an inability to sweat. Genetic testing reveals a mutation in the EDA gene. The embryologic basis for this child's combined symptoms is that hair follicles, teeth, and sweat glands all share which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Derivation from the mesoderm and dependence on lateral plate signaling."},{"label":"B","text":"Derivation from the endoderm and dependence on Sonic Hedgehog (Shh) signaling."},{"label":"C","text":"Derivation from the neural crest cells."},{"label":"D","text":"Derivation from the surface ectoderm and dependence on epithelial-mesenchymal interactions."},{"label":"E","text":"Derivation from the neuroectoderm and dependence on Wnt signaling."}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"integ-quiz-2-15","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Hair follicles, teeth, and sweat glands involve ectodermal epithelium and reciprocal epithelial–mesenchymal signaling, explaining their combined involvement in ectodermal dysplasia."},{"source_number":"16","prompt":"The EDA gene provides instructions for Ectodysplasin A, a member of the TNF family. In the context of hair follicle induction, the binding of Ectodysplasin A to its receptor EDAR is crucial for:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Directly causing the epithelial cells to proliferate and form the hair peg."},{"label":"B","text":"Activating Sonic Hedgehog (Shh) in the dermal condensate."},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibiting BMP signaling to allow for placode formation."},{"label":"D","text":"Amplifying the signal from the epidermal placode to the underlying mesenchyme, enabling dermal condensate formation."},{"label":"E","text":"Initiating the Wnt/B-catenin pathway in the epidermal placode."}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"integ-quiz-2-16","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"EDA–EDAR signaling helps reinforce epithelial-to-mesenchymal signaling needed for dermal condensate/appendage development."},{"source_number":"17","prompt":"A patient with a chronic venous stasis ulcer on the lower leg is healing by secondary intention. Which of the following is a characteristic feature of this type of healing that distinguishes it from primary intention?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The wound edges are closely apposed, and minimal granulation tissue is formed."},{"label":"B","text":"There is a significant loss of tissue, and the wound heals from the base upwards with the formation of granulation tissue."},{"label":"C","text":"The process is faster and results in a stronger, more vascular scar."},{"label":"D","text":"The inflammatory phase is completely bypassed, and the wound heals by direct epithelialization."},{"label":"E","text":"The wound is closed primarily with sutures, and a scab forms within 24 hours."}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"integ-quiz-2-17","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Secondary intention involves substantial tissue loss, granulation tissue, contraction, and healing upward from the wound base."},{"source_number":"18","prompt":"A researcher is studying the effects of chronic UV exposure on human skin. They observe a significant increase in the activity of MMP-1 (collagenase-1). This increase is most directly attributed to the activation of which of the following pathways?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NF-kB signaling pathway"},{"label":"B","text":"TGF-B/SMAD signaling pathway"},{"label":"C","text":"MAPK/AP-1 signaling pathway"},{"label":"D","text":"Wnt/B-catenin signaling pathway"},{"label":"E","text":"JAK-STAT signaling pathway"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"integ-quiz-2-18","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"UV-generated oxidative stress activates MAPK/AP-1, increasing MMPs such as MMP-1 and accelerating collagen degradation in photoaging."},{"source_number":"19","prompt":"A patient with Dystrophic Epidermolysis Bullosa (DEB) heals with significant scarring and milia formation. This is in direct contrast to a patient with Junctional Epidermolysis Bullosa (JEB). What is the primary structural reason for the difference in healing outcomes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The inflammatory response is always more severe in DEB than in JEB."},{"label":"B","text":"Milia are a result of the specific protein deficiency in JEB, not DEB."},{"label":"C","text":"JEB is a milder disease with a better healing capacity."},{"label":"D","text":"DEB is caused by a defect in the epidermis, while JEB affects the dermis."},{"label":"E","text":"DEB involves a separation plane beneath the lamina densa, disrupting the dermal-epidermal interface at a deeper level WY and impairing regeneration."}],"type":"mcq","answer":"E","id":"integ-quiz-2-19","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"DEB separates below the lamina densa at the anchoring fibrils, producing deeper injury and greater scarring/milia than more superficial junctional cleavage."},{"source_number":"20","prompt":"A medical student asks why preterm infants cannot effectively utilize shivering thermogenesis as a compensatory mechanism for heat loss. The best response is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Preterm infants lack the skeletal muscle mass required for shivering"},{"label":"B","text":"Shivering thermogenesis is inhibited by the high humidity in the incubator"},{"label":"C","text":"The neurologic control centers for shivering are not fully developed until 40 weeks"},{"label":"D","text":"Preterm infants have an exaggerated shivering response that paradoxically increases heat loss"},{"label":"E","text":"Preterm infants have limited ability to use shivering thermogenesis effectively, and instead rely on non-shivering v thermogenesis via BAT, which is also underdeveloped"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"E","id":"integ-quiz-2-20","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Preterm infants have limited shivering capacity and rely heavily on non-shivering thermogenesis; brown adipose tissue reserves/function are also immature."},{"source_number":"21","prompt":"A patient with OCA1A has white-blonde hair and very pale skin. A patient with OCA2 (associated with a defect in the P protein, which is involved in melanosome pH regulation) typically has some pigment and may have yellow or light brown hair. Which of the following best explains the phenotypic difference at the cellular level?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"In OCA2, tyrosinase activity is completely normal, but the enzyme cannot exit the endoplasmic reticulum."},{"label":"B","text":"In OCA1A, there is a complete block in melanin synthesis, whereas in OCA2, the defect is in the melanosome v environment, allowing for some residual melanin production."},{"label":"C","text":"In OCA2, the defect is in melanoblast migration, resulting in patchy pigmentation."},{"label":"D","text":"In OCA2, melanosomes are larger and more numerous, allowing for some pigment deposition."},{"label":"E","text":"In OCA1A, the melanocytes are absent, whereas in OCA2, the melanocytes are present but have defective organelles. Vaiir anewor ic nnrrart"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"integ-quiz-2-21","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"OCA1A causes essentially complete failure of melanin synthesis, whereas OCA2 alters the melanosomal environment and often permits residual pigment production."},{"source_number":"22","prompt":"A patient with diabetes mellitus presents with a non-healing ulcer on the plantar surface of the foot. Which of the following systemic factors is most likely contributing to the impaired wound healing in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"An overactive immune system leading to chronic inflammation"},{"label":"B","text":"Poor blood supply and impaired immune function secondary to the disease. VW"},{"label":"C","text":"Excessive collagen production leading to keloid formation"},{"label":"D","text":"Increased epidermal cell proliferation causing hyperkeratosis"},{"label":"E","text":"Elevated levels of growth factors that inhibit angiogenesis"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"integ-quiz-2-22","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Diabetes impairs wound repair through vascular insufficiency, neuropathy, and dysfunctional host defense."},{"source_number":"23","prompt":"A 45-year-old patient presents with multiple well-demarcated, depigmented macules on the face and hands. The patient has no history of childhood nystagmus, and their visual acuity is normal. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis, and what is its underlying mechanism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Waardenburg Syndrome; defect in neural crest cell development"},{"label":"B","text":"Vitiligo; autoimmune destruction of melanocytes"},{"label":"C","text":"Albinism; defect in melanosome transfer to keratinocytes"},{"label":"D","text":"Piebaldism; defect in melanoblast migration"},{"label":"E","text":"Oculocutaneous Albinism Type 1; congenital defect in melanin synthesis"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"integ-quiz-2-23","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Acquired sharply demarcated depigmented macules with normal early visual development fit vitiligo, caused by autoimmune loss of melanocytes."},{"source_number":"24","prompt":"A neonatologist explains to a medical student that the epidermal barrier becomes functionally competent at a specific gestational age. At approximately what gestational age does the epidermis complete functional keratinization and form a competent barrier?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"24 weeks"},{"label":"B","text":"40 weeks"},{"label":"C","text":"34-36 weeks"},{"label":"D","text":"30-32 weeks %"},{"label":"E","text":"28 weeks"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"integ-quiz-2-24","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"A mature epidermal permeability barrier is achieved late in gestation, roughly around 34–36 weeks."},{"source_number":"25","prompt":"In a 28-week gestation infant, which of the following integumentary structures is underdeveloped and critically contributes to impaired non-shivering thermogenesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Eccrine sweat glands"},{"label":"B","text":"Hair follicles"},{"label":"C","text":"Sebaceous glands"},{"label":"D","text":"Dermal papillae"},{"label":"E","text":"Brown adipose tissue (BAT)"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"E","id":"integ-quiz-2-25","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Brown adipose tissue is central to neonatal non-shivering thermogenesis and is relatively limited/immature in very preterm infants."},{"source_number":"26","prompt":"A 25-year-old patient with deep partial-thickness burns over 30% of their body surface area is admitted to the burn unit. During the first 48 hours, which of the following represents the most immediate life-threatening complication that requires aggressive monitoring and intervention?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypertrophic scarring and keloid formation"},{"label":"B","text":"Dehydration and hypovolaemic shock"},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic wound infection with multidrug-resistant organisms"},{"label":"D","text":"Contracture formation leading to joint immobility"},{"label":"E","text":"Marjolin's ulcer development at the burn site"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"integ-quiz-2-26","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"During the first 24–48 hours after a major burn, capillary leak and evaporative losses can produce severe hypovolemia and shock."},{"source_number":"27","prompt":"A 35-year-old patient sustains deep partial-thickness burns to 40% of their total body surface area. Which of the following best explains the sequence of pathophysiologic events that leads to the development of burn shock?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epidermal destruction — increased evaporative water loss — hypernatremia — osmotic fluid shifts"},{"label":"B","text":"Cellular apoptosis — release of inflammatory mediators — vasoconstriction — increased afterload"},{"label":"C","text":"Increased cardiac output — decreased systemic vascular resistance — capillary leak — fluid overload"},{"label":"D","text":"Vasodilation — increased capillary permeability — third spacing of fluid > decreased intravascular volume — hypotension"},{"label":"E","text":"Coagulation necrosis — platelet aggregation — thrombosis — increased peripheral resistance"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"integ-quiz-2-27","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Burn shock follows systemic inflammation/vasodilation and increased capillary permeability, causing third spacing, reduced circulating volume, and hypotension."},{"source_number":"28","prompt":"The structural protein collagen XVII/BP180 acts as a transmembrane component of the hemidesmosome. A deficiency in this protein would most directly weaken the connection between which two structures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The keratin intermediate filaments and the hemidesmosomal plaque."},{"label":"B","text":"The hemidesmosomal plaque and the keratin intermediate filaments."},{"label":"C","text":"The lamina densa and the dermal collagen fibrils."},{"label":"D","text":"The basal keratinocyte and the anchoring filaments of the lamina lucida."},{"label":"E","text":"The lamina lucida and the lamina densa."}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"integ-quiz-2-28","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Collagen XVII (BP180) is a transmembrane hemidesmosomal protein that links basal keratinocytes to extracellular anchoring structures in the lamina lucida."},{"source_number":"29","prompt":"Which of the following statements correctly distinguishes the molecular mechanisms of intrinsic (chronological) skin aging from extrinsic (photo)aging?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intrinsic aging is driven primarily by UV-induced oxidative stress, while extrinsic aging is driven by genetic factors."},{"label":"B","text":"Intrinsic aging involves the accumulation of senescent cells and the SASP, while extrinsic aging primarily involves the v MAPK/AP-1 pathway leading to MMP-mediated collagen degradation"},{"label":"C","text":"Extrinsic aging results in the loss of melanocytes, while intrinsic aging results in hyperpigmentation"},{"label":"D","text":"Intrinsic aging is characterized by increased collagen synthesis, while extrinsic aging is characterized by decreased collagen synthesis"},{"label":"E","text":"Intrinsic aging is reversible, while extrinsic aging is irreversible"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"integ-quiz-2-29","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Intrinsic aging prominently involves cellular senescence/SASP, while photoaging activates MAPK/AP-1 and MMP-mediated extracellular-matrix breakdown."},{"source_number":"30","prompt":"Which of the following best explains why a 3-year-old boy with an EDA mutation is severely affected, while his mother, who carries the same mutation, may only have mild dental anomalies or no symptoms at all?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The son has a de novo mutation, while the mother is a somatic mosaic."},{"label":"B","text":"The mother's mutation is in a different gene than the son's mutation."},{"label":"C","text":"The son's mutation is homozygous, while the mother's mutation is heterozygous."},{"label":"D","text":"The mother's symptoms are suppressed by higher levels of estrogen."},{"label":"E","text":"The mother has two X chromosomes, allowing one normal copy to compensate, while the son has only one X chromosome."}],"type":"mcq","answer":"E","id":"integ-quiz-2-30","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"EDA-associated hypohidrotic ectodermal dysplasia is commonly X-linked recessive; females may be mild carriers because they have a second X chromosome."},{"source_number":"31","prompt":"Melanoblasts, the precursors to melanocytes, originate from the neural crest and migrate to the epidermis. Which of the following correctly describes their migration pathway and the timing of their arrival?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ventrolateral pathway through the somites, arriving in the epidermis by week 6."},{"label":"B","text":"Dorsolateral pathway between the dermomyotome and surface ectoderm, arriving by week 8."},{"label":"C","text":"Ventrolateral pathway along the neural tube, arriving in the epidermis by week 12."},{"label":"D","text":"Dorsolateral pathway through the developing gut mesentery, arriving by week 10."},{"label":"E","text":"Direct migration through the amniotic fluid and surface epithelium, arriving by week 4."}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"integ-quiz-2-31","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Melanoblasts use the dorsolateral neural crest migration pathway between dermomyotome and surface ectoderm and reach epidermis around week 8."},{"source_number":"32","prompt":"A 70-year-old patient complains of dry, itchy skin (xerosis). The primary pathophysiological mechanism for this condition in the aging skin is","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epidermal atrophy and reduced lipid biosynthesis, leading to increased transepidermal water loss"},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased melanin production, making the skin more susceptible to UV damage"},{"label":"C","text":"Overactivity of the eccrine sweat glands, causing electrolyte depletion"},{"label":"D","text":"Increased sebum production leading to an oily skin surface"},{"label":"E","text":"Hyperplasia of the stratum corneum, creating a thick, impermeable barrier"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"integ-quiz-2-32","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Aging reduces epidermal lipid production and barrier efficiency, increasing transepidermal water loss and xerosis."},{"source_number":"33","prompt":"A 68-year-old patient is brought to the burn unit with full-thickness (third-degree) burns. The attending physician explains that the patient's prognosis is very poor. Applying the \"Rule of 80\" (age + TBSA), which of the following combinations would confer the most concerning prognosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"25% TBSA burn"},{"label":"B","text":"5% TBSA burn"},{"label":"C","text":"10% TBSA burn"},{"label":"D","text":"15% TBSA burn"},{"label":"E","text":"30% TBSA burn"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"E","id":"integ-quiz-2-33","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"Using the stated age+TBSA heuristic, 68 + 30 gives the highest total among the choices and therefore the most concerning prognosis."},{"source_number":"34","prompt":"A patient with a clean, surgical incision undergoes primary intention healing. Which of the following correctly describes the sequence of cellular events during the proliferation phase of this process?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fibroblasts secrete collagen — Granulation tissue develops — Epithelial cells proliferate."},{"label":"B","text":"Epithelial cells proliferate across the wound — Granulation tissue develops — Fibroblasts continue to secrete collagen."},{"label":"C","text":"Granulation tissue develops — Fibroblasts secrete collagen — Epithelial cells proliferate."},{"label":"D","text":"Macrophages remove the clot — Epithelial cells proliferate — Granulation tissue develops."},{"label":"E","text":"Fibroblasts secrete collagen — Macrophages migrate into the clot — Epithelial cells proliferate across the wound."}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"integ-quiz-2-34","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"In proliferation, re-epithelialization begins early while granulation tissue forms and fibroblasts continue collagen/ECM deposition."},{"source_number":"35","prompt":"An infant presents with severe skin fragility and blistering. Genetic testing reveals a mutation in the LAMA3 gene. Based on the case discussion, which of the following is the most likely diagnosis and primary site of tissue separation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dystrophic Epidermolysis Bullosa (DEB); separation within the lamina lucida"},{"label":"B","text":"Junctional Epidermolysis Bullosa (JEB); separation within the lamina lucida"},{"label":"C","text":"Junctional Epidermolysis Bullosa (JEB); separation below the lamina densa"},{"label":"D","text":"Dystrophic Epidermolysis Bullosa (DEB); separation below the lamina densa"},{"label":"E","text":"Epidermolysis Bullosa Simplex (EBS); separation within the epidermis"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"integ-quiz-2-35","set_id":"integ-quiz-2","system":"Integumentary","key_status":"review","rationale":"LAMA3 encodes a laminin-332 subunit; defects cause junctional epidermolysis bullosa with cleavage in the lamina lucida."}]},{"id":"ms-source-keyed","system":"Musculoskeletal","title":"Musculoskeletal & Membrane Physiology Quiz","subtitle":"Transport, skeletal muscle & smooth muscle","source":"musculoskeletal-quiz.pdf","key_status":"source","questions":[{"source_number":"1","prompt":"This type of transport mechanism of solutes requires a carrier protein by a passive process:","type":"recall","options":[],"answer":"Facilitated diffusion","key_status":"source","id":"ms-source-keyed-1","set_id":"ms-source-keyed","system":"Musculoskeletal","rationale":"Facilitated diffusion is passive movement down an electrochemical gradient through a specific carrier or channel."},{"source_number":"2","prompt":"The resting membrane potential will become more negative if:","type":"recall","options":[],"answer":"Extracellular potassium is decreased.","key_status":"source","id":"ms-source-keyed-2","set_id":"ms-source-keyed","system":"Musculoskeletal","rationale":"Lower extracellular K+ increases the K+ concentration gradient out of the cell, making the resting membrane potential more negative."},{"source_number":"3","prompt":"During one complete catalytic cycle of the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase pump, what are the stoichiometric ratios of ions moved and ATP consumed?","type":"recall","options":[],"answer":"3 Na⁺ out, 2 K⁺ in, 1 ATP consumed","key_status":"source","id":"ms-source-keyed-3","set_id":"ms-source-keyed","system":"Musculoskeletal","rationale":"The Na+/K+-ATPase uses one ATP to move 3 Na+ out and 2 K+ into the cell."},{"source_number":"4","prompt":"The transport of glucose from the intestinal lumen into the enterocyte via SGLT1 is coupled with the electrochemical gradient of which ion?","type":"recall","options":[],"answer":"Na⁺","key_status":"source","id":"ms-source-keyed-4","set_id":"ms-source-keyed","system":"Musculoskeletal","rationale":"SGLT1 uses the inward Na+ gradient to drive secondary active uptake of glucose."},{"source_number":"5","prompt":"Which of the following would likely move through the lipid bilayer of a plasma membrane most rapidly?","type":"recall","options":[],"answer":"CO2","key_status":"source","id":"ms-source-keyed-5","set_id":"ms-source-keyed","system":"Musculoskeletal","rationale":"CO2 is small and nonpolar, so it diffuses rapidly through the lipid bilayer."},{"source_number":"6","prompt":"In a resting skeletal muscle fiber, which of the following prevents myosin from binding to actin?","type":"recall","options":[],"answer":"Tropomyosin covers the myosin-binding sites on actin.","key_status":"source","id":"ms-source-keyed-6","set_id":"ms-source-keyed","system":"Musculoskeletal","rationale":"Tropomyosin blocks myosin-binding sites on actin at rest until Ca2+-troponin signaling moves it away."},{"source_number":"7","prompt":"A forensic pathologist notes that a deceased patient’s skeletal muscles are rigid several hours post-mortem. Why are the cross-bridges locked?","type":"recall","options":[],"answer":"Depletion of cellular ATP prevents the detachment step of the cross-bridge.","key_status":"source","id":"ms-source-keyed-7","set_id":"ms-source-keyed","system":"Musculoskeletal","rationale":"ATP is required for myosin to detach from actin; ATP depletion after death locks cross-bridges and produces rigor mortis."},{"source_number":"8","prompt":"Which molecular event directly causes opening of RyR1 channels in the terminal cisternae of skeletal muscle sarcoplasmic reticulum?","type":"recall","options":[],"answer":"Mechanical coupling/conformational change of Dihydropyridine Receptors in the T-tubules","key_status":"source","id":"ms-source-keyed-8","set_id":"ms-source-keyed","system":"Musculoskeletal","rationale":"In skeletal muscle, depolarization changes the conformation of the DHPR, which mechanically opens RyR1 on the sarcoplasmic reticulum."},{"source_number":"9","prompt":"What is the direct biochemical consequence immediately following binding of a fresh ATP molecule to the myosin head during the cross-bridge cycle?","type":"recall","options":[],"answer":"The affinity of myosin for actin drops sharply, causing detachment of the cross-bridge.","key_status":"source","id":"ms-source-keyed-9","set_id":"ms-source-keyed","system":"Musculoskeletal","rationale":"ATP binding lowers myosin's affinity for actin and causes cross-bridge detachment before ATP hydrolysis re-cocks the head."},{"source_number":"10","prompt":"Muscle relaxation requires active transport of Ca²⁺ back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Which transporter accomplishes this?","type":"recall","options":[],"answer":"Sarcoplasmic/Endoplasmic Reticulum Calcium ATPase (SERCA)","key_status":"source","id":"ms-source-keyed-10","set_id":"ms-source-keyed","system":"Musculoskeletal","rationale":"SERCA actively pumps cytosolic Ca2+ back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum, allowing relaxation."},{"source_number":"11","prompt":"In smooth muscle, elevated cytoplasmic Ca²⁺ binds to which regulatory protein to initiate contraction?","type":"recall","options":[],"answer":"Calmodulin","key_status":"source","id":"ms-source-keyed-11","set_id":"ms-source-keyed","system":"Musculoskeletal","rationale":"Smooth-muscle Ca2+ binds calmodulin, which activates MLCK and promotes myosin light-chain phosphorylation."},{"source_number":"12","prompt":"What is the basis of contraction in smooth muscle physiology?","type":"recall","options":[],"answer":"Arrangement of actin and myosin filaments","key_status":"source","id":"ms-source-keyed-12","set_id":"ms-source-keyed","system":"Musculoskeletal","rationale":"Smooth muscle contracts through interaction of actin and myosin filaments despite lacking organized sarcomeres."},{"source_number":"13","prompt":"What is the role of calcium ions in smooth muscle contraction?","type":"recall","options":[],"answer":"Initiating stimulus for contraction","key_status":"source","id":"ms-source-keyed-13","set_id":"ms-source-keyed","system":"Musculoskeletal","rationale":"An increase in cytosolic Ca2+ is the key initiating signal that activates the calmodulin–MLCK pathway."},{"source_number":"14","prompt":"How is smooth muscle relaxation initiated in response to hormonal factors?","type":"recall","options":[],"answer":"Opening of potassium channels","key_status":"source","id":"ms-source-keyed-14","set_id":"ms-source-keyed","system":"Musculoskeletal","rationale":"Opening K+ channels hyperpolarizes smooth muscle and decreases Ca2+ entry, favoring relaxation."},{"source_number":"15","prompt":"What is the latch state in smooth muscle physiology?","type":"recall","options":[],"answer":"Sustained contraction with reduced ATP utilization","key_status":"source","id":"ms-source-keyed-15","set_id":"ms-source-keyed","system":"Musculoskeletal","rationale":"The latch state permits sustained force with slow cross-bridge cycling and low ATP consumption."},{"source_number":"16","prompt":"In what way are cardiac muscles and skeletal muscles similar?","type":"recall","options":[],"answer":"Both have striations","key_status":"source","id":"ms-source-keyed-16","set_id":"ms-source-keyed","system":"Musculoskeletal","rationale":"Both cardiac and skeletal muscle contain sarcomeres and therefore appear striated."},{"source_number":"17","prompt":"Contraction velocity of smooth muscle can be increased by:","type":"recall","options":[],"answer":"Phosphorylation of the myosin light chain","key_status":"source","id":"ms-source-keyed-17","set_id":"ms-source-keyed","system":"Musculoskeletal","rationale":"Greater phosphorylation of the myosin regulatory light chain increases cross-bridge cycling and contraction velocity."},{"source_number":"18","prompt":"Contraction of smooth muscle can occur in the absence of a change in membrane potential via:","type":"recall","options":[],"answer":"Stimulation of a receptor which increases IP3","key_status":"source","id":"ms-source-keyed-18","set_id":"ms-source-keyed","system":"Musculoskeletal","rationale":"Receptor-mediated IP3 production can release Ca2+ from intracellular stores without requiring an initial membrane-potential change."},{"source_number":"VR1","prompt":"Serum calcium; membrane excitability","type":"mcq","options":[{"label":"A","text":"An increase in statement 1 would cause an increase in statement 2"},{"label":"B","text":"An increase in statement 1 would cause a decrease in statement 2"},{"label":"C","text":"No relationship or no change"}],"answer":"B","key_status":"source","id":"ms-source-keyed-19","set_id":"ms-source-keyed","system":"Musculoskeletal","rationale":"Raising extracellular/serum Ca2+ stabilizes excitable membranes and decreases excitability, so the variables are inversely related."},{"source_number":"VR2","prompt":"Serum potassium; resting membrane potential negativity","type":"mcq","options":[{"label":"A","text":"An increase in statement 1 would cause an increase in statement 2"},{"label":"B","text":"An increase in statement 1 would cause a decrease in statement 2"},{"label":"C","text":"No relationship or no change"}],"answer":"B","key_status":"source","id":"ms-source-keyed-20","set_id":"ms-source-keyed","system":"Musculoskeletal","rationale":"Higher extracellular K+ makes the K+ equilibrium potential less negative, so resting membrane potential becomes less negative; 'negativity' decreases."},{"source_number":"VR3","prompt":"Suprathreshold stimulus; amplitude of action potential","type":"mcq","options":[{"label":"A","text":"An increase in statement 1 would cause an increase in statement 2"},{"label":"B","text":"An increase in statement 1 would cause a decrease in statement 2"},{"label":"C","text":"No relationship or no change"}],"answer":"C","key_status":"source","id":"ms-source-keyed-21","set_id":"ms-source-keyed","system":"Musculoskeletal","rationale":"Once threshold is reached, action-potential amplitude is largely all-or-none; increasing a suprathreshold stimulus does not proportionally increase amplitude."},{"source_number":"VR4","prompt":"Sarcomeres in series; contraction velocity","type":"mcq","options":[{"label":"A","text":"An increase in statement 1 would cause an increase in statement 2"},{"label":"B","text":"An increase in statement 1 would cause a decrease in statement 2"},{"label":"C","text":"No relationship or no change"}],"answer":"A","key_status":"source","id":"ms-source-keyed-22","set_id":"ms-source-keyed","system":"Musculoskeletal","rationale":"More sarcomeres in series permit greater shortening distance/velocity, so the variables increase together."},{"source_number":"VR5","prompt":"MLCP; smooth muscle contraction","type":"mcq","options":[{"label":"A","text":"An increase in statement 1 would cause an increase in statement 2"},{"label":"B","text":"An increase in statement 1 would cause a decrease in statement 2"},{"label":"C","text":"No relationship or no change"}],"answer":"B","key_status":"source","id":"ms-source-keyed-23","set_id":"ms-source-keyed","system":"Musculoskeletal","rationale":"MLCP dephosphorylates myosin light chains and promotes smooth-muscle relaxation, so more MLCP means less contraction."},{"source_number":"VR6","prompt":"Serum calcium; smooth muscle contraction","type":"mcq","options":[{"label":"A","text":"An increase in statement 1 would cause an increase in statement 2"},{"label":"B","text":"An increase in statement 1 would cause a decrease in statement 2"},{"label":"C","text":"No relationship or no change"}],"answer":"A","key_status":"source","id":"ms-source-keyed-24","set_id":"ms-source-keyed","system":"Musculoskeletal","rationale":"More available Ca2+ promotes smooth-muscle contraction through calmodulin and MLCK."},{"source_number":"MTF1","prompt":"Statement 1: Voltage-gated Na⁺ channels have 3 gates.\nStatement 2: Voltage-gated K⁺ channels have only 1 activation gate.","type":"mcq","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Both statements are true"},{"label":"B","text":"Both statements are false"},{"label":"C","text":"Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false"},{"label":"D","text":"Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"}],"answer":"D","key_status":"source","id":"ms-source-keyed-25","set_id":"ms-source-keyed","system":"Musculoskeletal","rationale":"Voltage-gated Na+ channels have activation and inactivation gating rather than 'three gates,' whereas delayed-rectifier K+ channels are classically described with an activation gate; the source therefore marks statement 1 false and statement 2 true."},{"source_number":"MTF2","prompt":"Statement 1: When a stimulus reaches threshold, an action potential can reverse membrane polarity and become momentarily positive.\nStatement 2: Rapid depolarization is associated with a rapid increase in Na⁺ permeability and rapid opening of Na⁺ channels.","type":"mcq","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Both statements are true"},{"label":"B","text":"Both statements are false"},{"label":"C","text":"Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false"},{"label":"D","text":"Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"}],"answer":"A","key_status":"source","id":"ms-source-keyed-26","set_id":"ms-source-keyed","system":"Musculoskeletal","rationale":"At threshold, rapid Na+ channel opening drives depolarization and membrane-potential reversal; both statements are true."},{"source_number":"MTF3","prompt":"Statement 1: At the shortest biceps length, opposing actin filaments overlap and the actin–myosin relationship is distorted, so less force is generated.\nStatement 2: As actin filaments move completely out of myosin filaments, force of contraction increases.","type":"mcq","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Both statements are true"},{"label":"B","text":"Both statements are false"},{"label":"C","text":"Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false"},{"label":"D","text":"Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"}],"answer":"C","key_status":"source","id":"ms-source-keyed-27","set_id":"ms-source-keyed","system":"Musculoskeletal","rationale":"Very short sarcomeres lose optimal filament geometry and generate less force, but force also falls when actin no longer overlaps myosin; therefore statement 1 is true and statement 2 false."},{"source_number":"MTF4","prompt":"Statement 1: In skeletal muscle, a more perpendicular fiber orientation relative to the central tendon generates more force.\nStatement 2: The greater the load on skeletal muscle, the slower the contraction velocity.","type":"mcq","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Both statements are true"},{"label":"B","text":"Both statements are false"},{"label":"C","text":"Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false"},{"label":"D","text":"Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"}],"answer":"A","key_status":"source","id":"ms-source-keyed-28","set_id":"ms-source-keyed","system":"Musculoskeletal","rationale":"Pennate/perpendicular fiber architecture packs more fibers in parallel and increases force; increasing afterload slows shortening velocity. Both statements are true."},{"source_number":"MTF5","prompt":"Statement 1: In smooth muscle contraction, most Ca²⁺ needed for contraction enters from extracellular fluid.\nStatement 2: Transverse (T) tubules are present in smooth muscle.","type":"mcq","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Both statements are true"},{"label":"B","text":"Both statements are false"},{"label":"C","text":"Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false"},{"label":"D","text":"Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"}],"answer":"A","key_status":"source","id":"ms-source-keyed-29","set_id":"ms-source-keyed","system":"Musculoskeletal","rationale":"Smooth muscle relies heavily on extracellular Ca2+ entry. The source also marks the T-tubule statement true; note that standard histology/physiology usually describes smooth muscle as lacking true T-tubules and using caveolae, so confirm this item with your course source.","key_note":"Source answer preserved. Standard references generally state smooth muscle lacks true T-tubules and uses caveolae; review your instructor's intended framing."},{"source_number":"MTF6","prompt":"Statement 1: Smooth muscle has an overlapping pattern of actin and myosin similar to skeletal muscle.\nStatement 2: Smooth muscle does not consist of actin or myosin.","type":"mcq","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Both statements are true"},{"label":"B","text":"Both statements are false"},{"label":"C","text":"Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false"},{"label":"D","text":"Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"}],"answer":"C","key_status":"source","id":"ms-source-keyed-30","set_id":"ms-source-keyed","system":"Musculoskeletal","rationale":"Smooth muscle contains actin and myosin, so saying it has no actin/myosin is false; the source key marks the first statement true and second false."}]},{"id":"biochem-ec-ic","system":"Musculoskeletal","title":"Doc Charlie — Biochemistry of EC & IC Communication","subtitle":"ECM, membrane transport & cellular trafficking","source":"biochem-ec-ic.docx","key_status":"not_provided","questions":[{"source_number":"1","prompt":"Which amino acid is an absolute requirement at every third position of the collagen triple helix to allow for proper packing of the core?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lysine"},{"label":"B","text":"Proline"},{"label":"C","text":"Glycine"},{"label":"D","text":"None of these"},{"label":"E","text":"Hydroxyproline"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"biochem-ec-ic-1","set_id":"biochem-ec-ic","system":"Musculoskeletal","key_status":"review","rationale":"Glycine is required at every third residue of collagen because its tiny side chain fits into the tightly packed center of the triple helix."},{"source_number":"2","prompt":"A researcher is studying the fluidity of a synthetic lipid bilayer. How would the introduction of several double bonds in the cis configuration into the fatty acid tails affect the transition temperature (Tm)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The Tm would decrease."},{"label":"B","text":"The Tm would would remain unchanged."},{"label":"C","text":"The Tm would would increase."},{"label":"D","text":"The Tm would would initially increase then stabilize."},{"label":"E","text":"None of these"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"biochem-ec-ic-2","set_id":"biochem-ec-ic","system":"Musculoskeletal","key_status":"review","rationale":"Cis double bonds introduce kinks, reduce packing efficiency, and lower the membrane transition temperature."},{"source_number":"3","prompt":"During the formation of transport vesicles, which protein is responsible for the specific selection of cargo and the curvature of the membrane for vesicles moving from the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) to the Golgi apparatus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clathrin"},{"label":"B","text":"COPII"},{"label":"C","text":"Ankyrin"},{"label":"D","text":"None of these"},{"label":"E","text":"COPI"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"biochem-ec-ic-3","set_id":"biochem-ec-ic","system":"Musculoskeletal","key_status":"review","rationale":"COPII coats mediate cargo selection and vesicle budding from the ER toward the Golgi."},{"source_number":"4","prompt":"The 'ping-pong' model is often used to describe the mechanism of facilitated diffusion. Which statement accurately distinguishes this process from simple diffusion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"None of these"},{"label":"B","text":"Facilitated diffusion moves solutes against their electrochemical gradient."},{"label":"C","text":"The rate of facilitated diffusion is always slower than simple diffusion."},{"label":"D","text":"Facilitated diffusion is saturable and exhibits a Vmax"},{"label":"E","text":"Facilitated diffusion does not require a specific protein carrier."}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"biochem-ec-ic-4","set_id":"biochem-ec-ic","system":"Musculoskeletal","key_status":"review","rationale":"Carrier-mediated facilitated diffusion is passive but saturable because a finite number of transporters creates a Vmax."},{"source_number":"5","prompt":"What is the primary role of the chaperone protein BiP (binding immunoglobulin protein) within the lumen of the endoplasmic reticulum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acting as a GEF to switch Rab proteins to their GTP-bound state."},{"label":"B","text":"Exporting misfolded proteins through the nuclear pore complex."},{"label":"C","text":"Promoting proper protein folding and preventing aggregation."},{"label":"D","text":"None of these"},{"label":"E","text":"Cleaving the N-terminal signal peptide from nascent proteins."}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"biochem-ec-ic-5","set_id":"biochem-ec-ic","system":"Musculoskeletal","key_status":"review","rationale":"BiP is an ER chaperone that binds nascent/misfolded proteins to promote folding and prevent aggregation."},{"source_number":"6","prompt":"Which component of the extracellular matrix is a large, multidomain glycoprotein that binds to integrin receptors via an RGD (Arg-Gly-Asp) sequence, effectively linking the ECM to the actin cytoskeleton?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fibronectin"},{"label":"B","text":"Elastin"},{"label":"C","text":"None"},{"label":"D","text":"Hyaluronic acid"},{"label":"E","text":"Fibrillin-1"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"biochem-ec-ic-6","set_id":"biochem-ec-ic","system":"Musculoskeletal","key_status":"review","rationale":"Fibronectin contains an RGD motif that binds integrins, linking extracellular matrix to the actin cytoskeleton."},{"source_number":"7","prompt":"Which of the following describes the unique characteristics of Glycosaminoglycans (GAGs) found in proteoglycan complexes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They are attached to the core protein via strong noncovalent hydrogen bonds."},{"label":"B","text":"None"},{"label":"C","text":"They are unbranched polysaccharides composed of repeating disaccharide units."},{"label":"D","text":"They lack sulfate groups, making them electrically neutral."},{"label":"E","text":"They are primarily composed of hydrophobic amino acids to allow for ECM stability."}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"biochem-ec-ic-7","set_id":"biochem-ec-ic","system":"Musculoskeletal","key_status":"review","rationale":"GAGs are long, unbranched polysaccharides built from repeating disaccharide units and are usually highly negatively charged."},{"source_number":"8","prompt":"In the process of vesicle fusion, what is the role of the v-SNARE and t-SNARE proteins?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They hydrolyze ATP to move vesicles along microtubule tracks."},{"label":"B","text":"None"},{"label":"C","text":"They provide the GTPase activity needed for vesicle budding."},{"label":"D","text":"They act as molecular chaperones to prevent protein misfolding."},{"label":"E","text":"They interact with each other to mediate vesicle-membrane fusion."}],"type":"mcq","answer":"E","id":"biochem-ec-ic-8","set_id":"biochem-ec-ic","system":"Musculoskeletal","key_status":"review","rationale":"Complementary v-SNARE and t-SNARE proteins pair to bring vesicle and target membranes together for fusion."},{"source_number":"9","prompt":"A protein intended for the peroxisomal matrix is synthesized on a cytosolic polyribosome. Which signal sequence is most likely present at its carboxyl terminal?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"PTS1 (Ser-Lys-Leu)"},{"label":"B","text":"KDEL (Lys-Asp-Glu-Leu)"},{"label":"C","text":"NLS (Pro2-Lys3-Arg-Lys-Val)"},{"label":"D","text":"Mannose 6-phosphate"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"biochem-ec-ic-9","set_id":"biochem-ec-ic","system":"Musculoskeletal","key_status":"review","rationale":"Most peroxisomal matrix proteins use a C-terminal PTS1 signal, classically Ser-Lys-Leu (SKL)."},{"source_number":"10","prompt":"The asymmetry of the plasma membrane is maintained by the fact that certain phospholipids are preferentially located in specific leaflets. Which phospholipids are mainly found in the outer leaflet?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phosphatidylserine and phosphatidylethanolamine"},{"label":"B","text":"Cardiolipin and phosphatidylinositol"},{"label":"C","text":"Phosphatidylcholine and sphingomyelin"},{"label":"D","text":"Cholesterol and glycolipids"},{"label":"E","text":"None"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"biochem-ec-ic-10","set_id":"biochem-ec-ic","system":"Musculoskeletal","key_status":"review","rationale":"Phosphatidylcholine and sphingomyelin are enriched in the outer leaflet; phosphatidylserine and phosphatidylethanolamine are mainly inner-leaflet lipids."}]},{"id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","title":"Doc Ade — Immune System Quiz","subtitle":"Embryology of the Immune System","source":"immune-embryology.docx","key_status":"not_provided","questions":[{"source_number":"1","prompt":"A fetus has a defect that prevents formation of the lymph sacs and developing lymphatic vessels. Development of which lymphoid structure is most directly impaired?","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Bone marrow"},{"label":"2","text":"Lymph nodes"},{"label":"3","text":"Thymus"},{"label":"4","text":"Spleen"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"2","id":"immune-embryo-1","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Lymph nodes form in association with developing lymphatic vessels/sacs, so failure of these structures most directly impairs lymph-node development."},{"source_number":"2","prompt":"Lymph nodes develop from:","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Blood islands of the yolk sac"},{"label":"2","text":"Endodermal buds of the primitive gut tube"},{"label":"3","text":"Outgrowths of the third pharyngeal pouch"},{"label":"4","text":"Mesenchymal condensations along developing lymphatic vessels and lymph sacs"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"4","id":"immune-embryo-2","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Lymph nodes arise when mesenchyme condenses around developing lymphatic sacs and vessels and is later colonized by lymphocytes."},{"source_number":"3","prompt":"The palatine tonsil is derived from which pharyngeal pouch?","options":[{"label":"1","text":"First"},{"label":"2","text":"Third"},{"label":"3","text":"Fourth"},{"label":"4","text":"Second"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"4","id":"immune-embryo-3","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Palatine tonsillar epithelium derives from the second pharyngeal pouch."},{"source_number":"4","prompt":"DiGeorge syndrome is caused by a microdeletion at chromosome:","options":[{"label":"1","text":"15q11–q13"},{"label":"2","text":"22q11.2"},{"label":"3","text":"Xq21.3"},{"label":"4","text":"7q11.23"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"2","id":"immune-embryo-4","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"DiGeorge syndrome is classically associated with a 22q11.2 microdeletion."},{"source_number":"5","prompt":"In heterotaxy syndromes, congenital asplenia is classically associated with:","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Bilateral left-sidedness, with bilateral bilobed lungs"},{"label":"2","text":"Complete situs inversus with a normal spleen"},{"label":"3","text":"Bilateral right-sidedness, with bilateral trilobed lungs"},{"label":"4","text":"Biliary atresia with normal lung lobation"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"3","id":"immune-embryo-5","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Congenital asplenia is associated with right isomerism: bilateral right-sidedness and often bilateral trilobed lungs."},{"source_number":"6","prompt":"Which statement correctly contrasts early B-cell and T-cell development?","options":[{"label":"1","text":"B-cell progenitors migrate to lymph nodes, while T-cell progenitors remain in the marrow"},{"label":"2","text":"B-cell progenitors mature within blood-forming tissue, while T-cell progenitors migrate to the thymus"},{"label":"3","text":"Both lineages must migrate to the thymus to acquire their antigen receptors"},{"label":"4","text":"Both lineages complete their maturation within the bone marrow"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"2","id":"immune-embryo-6","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"B-lineage cells mature in hematopoietic tissue, whereas T-cell progenitors leave blood-forming sites and seed the thymus."},{"source_number":"7","prompt":"A 5-day-old infant has tetany, a low serum calcium level, truncus arteriosus, and a cleft palate. Which single embryologic lesion best explains all of these findings?","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Disrupted development of the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches and the associated neural crest"},{"label":"2","text":"Failure of definitive stem cells to emerge from the AGM region"},{"label":"3","text":"Failure of the first and second pharyngeal pouches to form"},{"label":"4","text":"Abnormal left–right patterning of the body axis"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"1","id":"immune-embryo-7","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"DiGeorge findings reflect abnormal third/fourth pouch development plus neural crest defects, explaining hypocalcemia, thymic deficiency, and conotruncal defects."},{"source_number":"8","prompt":"The parafollicular (C) cells of the thyroid are incorporated into the gland by way of the:","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Dorsal wings of the fourth pharyngeal pouch"},{"label":"2","text":"Tubotympanic recess of the first pharyngeal pouch"},{"label":"3","text":"Dorsal wings of the third pharyngeal pouch"},{"label":"4","text":"Ultimobranchial body from the ventral wings of the fourth pharyngeal pouch"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"4","id":"immune-embryo-8","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Thyroid C-cell precursors are incorporated through the ultimobranchial body derived from the fourth pouch complex."},{"source_number":"9","prompt":"Medullary hematopoiesis begins at about week 12. This timing coincides with:","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Formation of the earliest ossification centers and their marrow cavities"},{"label":"2","text":"Budding of the thymic primordia from the third pharyngeal pouch"},{"label":"3","text":"Onset of thymic involution"},{"label":"4","text":"Appearance of blood islands in the wall of the yolk sac"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"1","id":"immune-embryo-9","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Bone-marrow hematopoiesis begins when ossification creates marrow cavities, around the end of the first trimester."},{"source_number":"10","prompt":"Which description best _ts hematopoietic activity in a normal embryo at week 7?","options":[{"label":"1","text":"The liver and bone marrow contribute equally, and the yolk sac is inactive"},{"label":"2","text":"Yolk sac and hepatic hematopoiesis overlap, and marrow hematopoiesis has not begun"},{"label":"3","text":"Hepatic hematopoiesis has not begun, and no definitive stem cells exist"},{"label":"4","text":"Yolk sac hematopoiesis has ended, and the bone marrow is the dominant site"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"2","id":"immune-embryo-10","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"At week 7, yolk-sac hematopoiesis is declining while hepatic hematopoiesis is active; marrow has not yet become a hematopoietic site."},{"source_number":"11","prompt":"Positive selection of thymocytes takes place mainly in the:","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Cortex, mediated by cortical thymic epithelial cells"},{"label":"2","text":"Capsule, mediated by neural crest-derived mesenchyme"},{"label":"3","text":"Medulla, mediated by medullary thymic epithelial cells"},{"label":"4","text":"Medulla, mediated by Hassall's corpuscles"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"1","id":"immune-embryo-11","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Positive selection occurs mainly in the thymic cortex through interaction with cortical thymic epithelial cells."},{"source_number":"12","prompt":"Which statement about the mesoblastic (yolk sac) phase of hematopoiesis is correct?","options":[{"label":"1","text":"It is transient and produces mostly primitive erythroblasts"},{"label":"2","text":"It is the lasting source of definitive hematopoietic stem cells"},{"label":"3","text":"It begins at about week 12, as ossification centers form"},{"label":"4","text":"It produces mainly granulocytes and early lymphocytes"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"1","id":"immune-embryo-12","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"The yolk-sac phase is transient and produces predominantly primitive erythroid cells."},{"source_number":"13","prompt":"An infant with SCID has a loss-of-function mutation in RAG1. Considering the role of this enzyme in lymphocyte development, which lymphocyte pro_le is expected?","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Normal T cells, absent B cells, normal NK cells"},{"label":"2","text":"Absent T cells, absent B cells, normal NK cells"},{"label":"3","text":"Absent T cells, normal B cells, absent NK cells"},{"label":"4","text":"Absent T cells, absent B cells, absent NK cells"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"2","id":"immune-embryo-13","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"RAG1 deficiency blocks V(D)J recombination in both T and B cells while sparing NK cells: T−B−NK+ SCID."},{"source_number":"14","prompt":"A 7-month-old boy has had three episodes of otitis media and one pneumococcal pneumonia. He was well until 6 months of age. No tonsillar tissue is visible. Serum IgG, IgA, and IgM are all markedly low, and T-cell counts are","options":[{"label":"1","text":"His thymus was still producing protective T cells"},{"label":"2","text":"Transplacental maternal IgG protected him until it waned"},{"label":"3","text":"Encapsulated bacteria do not infect infants younger than 6 months"},{"label":"4","text":"B cells made in the fetal liver persisted until 6 months"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"2","id":"immune-embryo-14","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Maternal IgG crosses the placenta and temporarily protects infants with defective antibody production until it wanes over the first months."},{"source_number":"15","prompt":"X-linked severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID) results from a mutation in the gene encoding:","options":[{"label":"1","text":"The common γ chain shared by several cytokine receptors"},{"label":"2","text":"Bruton tyrosine kinase"},{"label":"3","text":"The autoimmune regulator (AIRE)"},{"label":"4","text":"Adenosine deaminase"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"1","id":"immune-embryo-15","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"X-linked SCID most often involves the common gamma chain shared by several cytokine receptors."},{"source_number":"16","prompt":"The capsule and connective tissue septa of the thymus are derived from:","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Mesenchyme of the dorsal mesogastrium"},{"label":"2","text":"Hematopoietic precursors from the fetal liver"},{"label":"3","text":"Neural crest-derived mesenchyme of the pharyngeal arches"},{"label":"4","text":"Endoderm of the third pharyngeal pouch"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"3","id":"immune-embryo-16","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"The thymic capsule/septa come from surrounding neural crest-derived mesenchyme; thymic epithelial stroma itself is pouch endoderm."},{"source_number":"17","prompt":"In the case of SD, ( previous item),which routine vaccine in the Philippine infant immunization schedule poses the greatest risk?","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Hepatitis B vaccine"},{"label":"2","text":"Pneumococcal conjugate vaccine"},{"label":"3","text":"BCG vaccine"},{"label":"4","text":"Inactivated polio vaccine"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"3","id":"immune-embryo-17","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"BCG is a live vaccine and poses major risk in severe T-cell immunodeficiency such as SCID."},{"source_number":"18","prompt":"A child has a loss-of-function mutation in AIRE. Thymic size and peripheral T-cell counts are normal. Which consequence is most expected?","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Escape of self-reactive T cells, with autoimmunity affecting multiple organs"},{"label":"2","text":"Severe opportunistic infections from absent T cells"},{"label":"3","text":"Arrest of B-cell development at the pre-B stage"},{"label":"4","text":"Failure of thymocytes to recognize self-MHC"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"1","id":"immune-embryo-18","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"AIRE enables medullary thymic epithelial cells to display tissue-restricted self-antigens; loss allows autoreactive T cells to escape deletion."},{"source_number":"19","prompt":"A newborn with complex cyanotic congenital heart disease has a midline liver, no spleen on abdominal ultrasound, and bilateral trilobed lungs. Which peripheral blood smear finding is expected?","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Howell-Jolly bodies in red blood cells"},{"label":"2","text":"Absence of lymphocytes"},{"label":"3","text":"Large granular lymphocytes"},{"label":"4","text":"Primitive nucleated erythroblasts only"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"1","id":"immune-embryo-19","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Without splenic function, nuclear remnants persist in erythrocytes, producing Howell–Jolly bodies."},{"source_number":"20","prompt":"The thymocytes that populate the thymic primordium at about weeks 7–8 are derived from:","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Blood-borne hematopoietic precursors from the fetal liver"},{"label":"2","text":"Neural crest cells of the pharyngeal arches"},{"label":"3","text":"Endoderm of the third pharyngeal pouch"},{"label":"4","text":"Mesenchyme of the developing lymph sacs"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"1","id":"immune-embryo-20","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Thymocytes are hematopoietic cells delivered by blood-borne precursors from fetal hematopoietic sites such as the liver."},{"source_number":"21","prompt":"The first blood cells of the embryo appear in the:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aorta-gonad-mesonephros region"},{"label":"B","text":"Marrow cavity of the clavicle"},{"label":"C","text":"Blood islands in the wall of the yolk sac"},{"label":"D","text":"Liver"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"immune-embryo-21","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"The first embryonic blood cells arise in yolk-sac blood islands."},{"source_number":"22","prompt":"A thymocyte in the cortex expresses a T-cell receptor that cannot recognize self-MHC molecules. What is its fate?","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Death by neglect, from failed positive selection"},{"label":"2","text":"Differentiation into a regulatory T cell"},{"label":"3","text":"Export to the periphery as a naive T cell"},{"label":"4","text":"Deletion in the medulla by negative selection"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"1","id":"immune-embryo-22","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Failure to recognize self-MHC means failure of positive selection, leading to death by neglect in the cortex."},{"source_number":"23","prompt":"The tubotympanic recess, which forms the epithelial lining of the middle ear cavity and the auditory tube, is derived from the:","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Second pharyngeal pouch"},{"label":"2","text":"Third pharyngeal pouch"},{"label":"3","text":"First pharyngeal pouch"},{"label":"4","text":"Fourth pharyngeal pouch"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"3","id":"immune-embryo-23","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"The tubotympanic recess is a first pharyngeal pouch derivative."},{"source_number":"24","prompt":"The fetal liver is transiently grouped with the primary lymphoid organs because it:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Generates the earliest B-lineage cells before the bone marrow takes over"},{"label":"B","text":"Carries out negative selection of T cells before the thymus forms"},{"label":"C","text":"Filters blood-borne antigens and forms germinal centers during fetal life"},{"label":"D","text":"Gives rise to the epithelial framework of the thymus"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"immune-embryo-24","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"The fetal liver generates early B-lineage cells before bone marrow takes over lymphopoiesis."},{"source_number":"25","prompt":"In an infant with DiGeorge syndrome, B-cell numbers are normal but antibody responses to vaccines are poor. The best explanation is that:","options":[{"label":"1","text":"B-cell maturation is arrested at the pre-B stage"},{"label":"2","text":"B cells require the thymus for immunoglobulin gene rearrangement"},{"label":"3","text":"B cells lack the T-cell help needed for effective antibody responses"},{"label":"4","text":"Maternal IgG blocks the infant's own antibody production"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"3","id":"immune-embryo-25","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"DiGeorge may leave B-cell numbers intact, but poor T-cell help impairs T-dependent antibody responses to vaccines."},{"source_number":"26","prompt":"In heterotaxy syndromes, congenital asplenia is classically associated with:","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Bilateral left-sidedness, with bilateral bilobed lungs"},{"label":"2","text":"Biliary atresia with normal lung lobation"},{"label":"3","text":"Complete situs inversus with a normal spleen"},{"label":"4","text":"Bilateral right-sidedness, with bilateral trilobed lungs"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"4","id":"immune-embryo-26","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Asplenia/right isomerism is associated with bilateral right-sided structures such as trilobed lungs."},{"source_number":"27","prompt":"The spleen develops from:","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Mesenchyme within the dorsal mesogastrium"},{"label":"2","text":"Endoderm of the foregut"},{"label":"3","text":"Endoderm of the second pharyngeal pouch"},{"label":"4","text":"Neural crest cells"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"1","id":"immune-embryo-27","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"The spleen develops from mesenchyme in the dorsal mesogastrium."},{"source_number":"28","prompt":"SD, is a 3-month-old girl has Pneumocystis pneumonia, persistent oral thrush, chronic diarrhea, and failure to thrive. Chest X-ray shows no thymic shadow. Her absolute lymphocyte count is very low, with markedly reduced T","options":[{"label":"1","text":"X-linked agammaglobulinemia"},{"label":"2","text":"Congenital asplenia"},{"label":"3","text":"Complete DiGeorge syndrome"},{"label":"4","text":"Severe combined immunodeficiency"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"4","id":"immune-embryo-28","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Pneumocystis, thrush, diarrhea, failure to thrive, absent thymic shadow, and severe lymphopenia strongly suggest SCID."},{"source_number":"29","prompt":"Polysplenia is classically associated with:","options":[{"label":"1","text":"A BTK mutation and absent tonsils"},{"label":"2","text":"Bilateral trilobed lungs and Howell-Jolly bodies"},{"label":"3","text":"A 22q11.2 deletion and hypocalcemia"},{"label":"4","text":"Bilateral bilobed lungs and biliary atresia"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"4","id":"immune-embryo-29","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Polysplenia is classically linked to left isomerism, including bilateral bilobed lungs and sometimes biliary abnormalities."},{"source_number":"30","prompt":"Self-renewing, definitive hematopoietic stem cells originate from the:","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Extraembryonic mesoderm of the yolk sac"},{"label":"2","text":"Mesenchyme of the dorsal mesogastrium"},{"label":"3","text":"Splanchnopleuric mesoderm of the aorta-gonad-mesonephros (AGM) region"},{"label":"4","text":"Endoderm of the hepatic diverticulum"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"3","id":"immune-embryo-30","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Definitive self-renewing hematopoietic stem cells arise from hemogenic endothelium in the AGM region."},{"source_number":"31","prompt":"During parathyroidectomy, the surgeon cannot find an inferior parathyroid gland in its usual position. Based on its embryologic migration, the most likely ectopic location is:","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Within or near the thymus in the anterior superior mediastinum"},{"label":"2","text":"Along the auditory tube"},{"label":"3","text":"Within the palatine tonsillar fossa"},{"label":"4","text":"Within the gastrosplenic ligament"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"1","id":"immune-embryo-31","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Inferior parathyroids descend with the thymus and may therefore be ectopic within/near thymic tissue in the mediastinum."},{"source_number":"32","prompt":"A chest CT scan of a healthy 65-year-old shows mostly fat in the anterior superior mediastinum where the thymus is expected, with no mass. The best interpretation is:","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Failure of thymic descent during embryonic life"},{"label":"2","text":"Normal age-related thymic involution"},{"label":"3","text":"Thymic aplasia consistent with a 22q11.2 deletion"},{"label":"4","text":"Fatty change caused by a defect in medullary selection"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"2","id":"immune-embryo-32","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Replacement of thymic tissue by fat in an older adult is normal age-related thymic involution."},{"source_number":"33","prompt":"A fetus is evaluated at 20 weeks' gestation. The dominant site of hematopoiesis at this age is the:","options":[{"label":"1","text":"thymus"},{"label":"2","text":"Yolk sac"},{"label":"3","text":"Liver"},{"label":"4","text":"Bone marrow"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"3","id":"immune-embryo-33","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"During midgestation the fetal liver is the dominant hematopoietic organ."},{"source_number":"34","prompt":"Which of the following is a primary (central) lymphoid organ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Peyer patches"},{"label":"B","text":"Thymus"},{"label":"C","text":"Spleen"},{"label":"D","text":"Palatine tonsil"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"immune-embryo-34","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"The thymus is a primary/central lymphoid organ where T cells mature."},{"source_number":"35","prompt":"The inferior parathyroid glands develop from the:","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Ventral wings of the fourth pharyngeal pouch"},{"label":"2","text":"Dorsal wings of the fourth pharyngeal pouch"},{"label":"3","text":"Dorsal wings of the third pharyngeal pouch"},{"label":"4","text":"Ventral wings of the third pharyngeal pouch"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"3","id":"immune-embryo-35","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Inferior parathyroids arise from the dorsal wing of the third pharyngeal pouch."},{"source_number":"36","prompt":"The principal immunologic function of secondary (peripheral) lymphoid organs is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antigen-dependent activation, proliferation, and differentiation of lymphocytes"},{"label":"B","text":"Positive and negative selection of developing T cells"},{"label":"C","text":"Initial rearrangement of antigen receptor genes"},{"label":"D","text":"Generation of lymphocytes from hematopoietic stem cells"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"immune-embryo-36","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Secondary lymphoid organs are sites of antigen-dependent activation, proliferation, and differentiation of mature lymphocytes."},{"source_number":"37","prompt":"Which finding would be shared by an infant with X-linked agammaglobulinemia and an infant with congenital asplenia?","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Undetectable serum immunoglobulins"},{"label":"2","text":"Susceptibility to infection with encapsulated bacteria"},{"label":"3","text":"Howell-Jolly bodies on peripheral blood smear"},{"label":"4","text":"Absent tonsils on examination"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"2","id":"immune-embryo-37","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Both X-linked agammaglobulinemia and asplenia strongly predispose to infections with encapsulated bacteria."},{"source_number":"38","prompt":"X-linked agammaglobulinemia results from a mutation in the gene encoding:","options":[{"label":"1","text":"The common γ chain of cytokine receptors"},{"label":"2","text":"RAG1"},{"label":"3","text":"The AIRE protein"},{"label":"4","text":"Bruton tyrosine kinase"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"4","id":"immune-embryo-38","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"X-linked agammaglobulinemia is caused by BTK mutations that arrest B-cell maturation."},{"source_number":"39","prompt":"The tonsils are small or absent in X-linked agammaglobulinemia because:","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Maternal IgG suppresses growth of tonsillar tissue"},{"label":"2","text":"The second pharyngeal pouch fails to form"},{"label":"3","text":"Tonsillar lymphoid tissue depends on mature B cells, which fail to develop"},{"label":"4","text":"T cells fail to colonize the tonsillar mesenchyme"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"3","id":"immune-embryo-39","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Without mature B cells, lymphoid follicles/tonsillar tissue are poorly developed in X-linked agammaglobulinemia."},{"source_number":"40","prompt":"The inferior parathyroid glands arise from a more cranial pouch than the superior parathyroids, yet they lie caudal to them. The best explanation is that:","options":[{"label":"1","text":"The fourth pouch migrates cranially after the thyroid descends"},{"label":"2","text":"The third and fourth pouches exchange positions during folding of the pharynx"},{"label":"3","text":"The descending thymus carries the third-pouch parathyroid primordia caudally"},{"label":"4","text":"The superior parathyroids are pulled upward by the ultimobranchial body"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"3","id":"immune-embryo-40","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"The thymus drags the third-pouch inferior parathyroid primordia caudally during descent, placing them below the fourth-pouch superior glands."},{"source_number":"41","prompt":"The AIRE gene contributes to central tolerance by:","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Promoting survival of thymocytes that recognize self-MHC"},{"label":"2","text":"Directing descent of the thymic primordia into the thorax"},{"label":"3","text":"Driving VDJ recombination of the T-cell receptor genes"},{"label":"4","text":"Enabling medullary epithelial cells to display peripheral tissue-restricted self-antigens"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"4","id":"immune-embryo-41","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"AIRE supports central tolerance by inducing ectopic expression of peripheral tissue antigens in medullary thymic epithelial cells."},{"source_number":"42","prompt":"Which statement best explains, on embryologic grounds, why lymph nodes are classified as secondary lymphoid organs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They arise from the blood islands of the yolk sac"},{"label":"B","text":"They are mesenchymal sites that are colonized by lymphocytes formed elsewhere"},{"label":"C","text":"They arise from pharyngeal pouch endoderm that generates lymphocytes in situ"},{"label":"D","text":"They are the sites where lymphocytes first acquire their antigen receptors"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"immune-embryo-42","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Lymph nodes are secondary organs because mesenchymal sites are colonized by lymphocytes generated and matured elsewhere."},{"source_number":"43","prompt":"The thymic epithelial cells that form the structural framework of the thymus are derived from:","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Neural crest"},{"label":"2","text":"Endoderm"},{"label":"3","text":"Splanchnic mesoderm"},{"label":"4","text":"Ectoderm"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"2","id":"immune-embryo-43","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Thymic epithelial cells are derived from pharyngeal pouch endoderm."},{"source_number":"44","prompt":"The thymus develops from the:","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Second pharyngeal pouch"},{"label":"2","text":"Dorsal wings of the third pharyngeal pouch"},{"label":"3","text":"Ventral wings of the fourth pharyngeal pouch"},{"label":"4","text":"Ventral wings of the third pharyngeal pouch"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"4","id":"immune-embryo-44","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"The thymus develops primarily from the ventral wings of the third pharyngeal pouches."},{"source_number":"45","prompt":"A neonatal autopsy specimen shows a lymphoid organ with an endoderm-derived epithelial framework, densely packed immature lymphocytes in its outer zone, and no germinal centers. Which additional finding is expected in","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Crypts lined by stratified squamous epithelium"},{"label":"B","text":"Hassall's corpuscles in the inner zone"},{"label":"C","text":"White pulp surrounding central arterioles"},{"label":"D","text":"A subcapsular sinus receiving afferent lymphatics"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"immune-embryo-45","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Hassall corpuscles are characteristic structures of the thymic medulla."},{"source_number":"46","prompt":"After completing its descent, the thymus comes to lie in the:","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Middle mediastinum, within the pericardium"},{"label":"2","text":"Posterior mediastinum, behind the esophagus"},{"label":"3","text":"Root of the neck, behind the thyroid gland"},{"label":"4","text":"Superior mediastinum, anterior to the great vessels"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"4","id":"immune-embryo-46","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"The thymus descends into the anterior/superior mediastinum, anterior to the great vessels."},{"source_number":"47","prompt":"Which feature best characterizes mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT), such as Peyer patches?","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Unencapsulated lymphoid aggregates beneath mucosal epithelium, ininfiltrated by lymphocytes"},{"label":"2","text":"Encapsulated lymphoid tissue arising along the lymph sacs"},{"label":"3","text":"An endodermal epithelial organ colonized by thymocytes"},{"label":"4","text":"Primary lymphoid tissue where antigen receptor genes are rearranged"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"1","id":"immune-embryo-47","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"MALT consists of largely unencapsulated lymphoid aggregates beneath mucosal epithelium."},{"source_number":"48","prompt":"In the mature spleen, immune responses to blood-borne antigens occur mainly in the:","options":[{"label":"1","text":"White pulp, the lymphoid tissue around central arterioles"},{"label":"2","text":"Splenic hilum"},{"label":"3","text":"Red pulp, which filters aged red blood cells"},{"label":"4","text":"Capsule and trabeculae"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"1","id":"immune-embryo-48","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"The splenic white pulp is lymphoid tissue organized around central arterioles and mediates responses to blood-borne antigens."},{"source_number":"49","prompt":"The conotruncal cardiac defects of DiGeorge syndrome are best explained by:","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Failure of fourth-pouch endoderm to form the aortic arch"},{"label":"2","text":"Hypocalcemia during the period of cardiac septation"},{"label":"3","text":"Disrupted neural crest contribution to septation of the cardiac outflow tract"},{"label":"4","text":"T-cell-mediated injury to the developing heart"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"3","id":"immune-embryo-49","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Conotruncal defects in DiGeorge reflect abnormal neural crest contribution to cardiac outflow-tract septation."},{"source_number":"50","prompt":"n humans, the functional equivalent of the avian bursa of Fabricius is the:","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Thymus"},{"label":"2","text":"Bone marrow"},{"label":"3","text":"Spleen"},{"label":"4","text":"Appendix"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"2","id":"immune-embryo-50","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"In humans, bone marrow serves the B-cell maturation role analogous to the avian bursa of Fabricius."},{"source_number":"51","prompt":"Which sequence correctly orders B-cell development in the bone marrow?","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Immature B → pro-B → pre-B → mature B"},{"label":"2","text":"Pro-B → pre-B → immature B → mature B"},{"label":"3","text":"Pre-B → pro-B → immature B → mature B"},{"label":"4","text":"Pro-B → immature B → pre-B → mature B"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"2","id":"immune-embryo-51","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Normal B-cell maturation proceeds pro-B → pre-B → immature B → mature B."},{"source_number":"52","prompt":"A woman's immune thrombocytopenic purpura relapses 2 years after splenectomy. Her peripheral blood smear shows no Howell-Jolly bodies. The most likely explanation is","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Regeneration of the spleen from gut endoderm"},{"label":"2","text":"An accessory spleen left near the splenic hilum"},{"label":"3","text":"Thymic hyperplasia producing autoreactive T cells"},{"label":"4","text":"Extramedullary hematopoiesis in the liver"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"2","id":"immune-embryo-52","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Return of splenic function after splenectomy can occur when an accessory spleen was left behind."},{"source_number":"53","prompt":"An embryo has selective failure of the ventral wings of the third pharyngeal pouch. All other pouch components and neural crest migration are normal. Which pattern is expected at birth?","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Normal T cells with absent inferior parathyroids"},{"label":"2","text":"Normal T cells with absent thyroid C cells"},{"label":"3","text":"T-cell deficiency with normal parathyroid tissue and normal C cells"},{"label":"4","text":"T-cell deficiency with hypocalcemia from absent superior parathyroids"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"3","id":"immune-embryo-53","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Selective failure of the ventral third pouch removes thymic development, causing T-cell deficiency while sparing dorsal third-pouch parathyroid tissue."},{"source_number":"54","prompt":"Rearrangement of the immunoglobulin genes (VDJ recombination) during B-cell maturation is mediated by:","options":[{"label":"1","text":"The AIRE protein"},{"label":"2","text":"RAG1 and RAG2"},{"label":"3","text":"Bruton tyrosine kinase"},{"label":"4","text":"Adenosine deaminase"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"2","id":"immune-embryo-54","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"RAG1/2 initiate V(D)J recombination by cutting antigen-receptor gene segments."},{"source_number":"55","prompt":"Which sequence correctly orders the phases of prenatal hematopoiesis?","options":[{"label":"1","text":"Hepatic → mesoblastic → medullary"},{"label":"2","text":"Medullary → hepatic → mesoblastic"},{"label":"3","text":"Mesoblastic → hepatic → medullary"},{"label":"4","text":"Mesoblastic → medullary → hepatic"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"3","id":"immune-embryo-55","set_id":"immune-embryo","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Prenatal hematopoiesis proceeds from mesoblastic (yolk sac) to hepatic and then medullary (bone marrow) phases."}]},{"id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","title":"Doc Charlie — Immunoglobulins Exam","subtitle":"Immunoglobulins, complement & antibody diversity","source":"immunoglobulins.docx","key_status":"not_provided","questions":[{"source_number":"1","prompt":"On cellulose acetate electrophoresis of serum, immunoglobulins are found chiefly in which fraction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Albumin"},{"label":"B","text":"Alpha-1 globulins"},{"label":"C","text":"Beta globulins"},{"label":"D","text":"Gamma globulins"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"immunoglobulins-1","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Serum immunoglobulins migrate chiefly in the gamma-globulin fraction on routine protein electrophoresis."},{"source_number":"2","prompt":"Unlike most plasma proteins, which are made mainly in the liver, the gamma globulins are synthesized by which cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepatocytes"},{"label":"B","text":"Lymphocytes"},{"label":"C","text":"Vascular endothelial cells"},{"label":"D","text":"Erythrocytes"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"immunoglobulins-2","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Gamma globulins are produced by B-lineage cells, especially differentiated plasma cells, rather than hepatocytes."},{"source_number":"3","prompt":"The complement system is best described as the:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"cell-mediated arm of the adaptive immune system"},{"label":"B","text":"humoral arm of the adaptive immune system"},{"label":"C","text":"humoral arm of the innate immune system"},{"label":"D","text":"cellular arm of the innate immune system"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"immunoglobulins-3","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Complement is a soluble humoral component of innate immunity."},{"source_number":"4","prompt":"Which cells originate from the thymus and mediate cell-mediated immune responses?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T lymphocytes"},{"label":"B","text":"B lymphocytes"},{"label":"C","text":"Plasma cells"},{"label":"D","text":"Neutrophils"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"immunoglobulins-4","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"T lymphocytes mature in the thymus and mediate major forms of cell-mediated adaptive immunity."},{"source_number":"5","prompt":"Which feature distinguishes the adaptive immune system from the innate immune system?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Its components are fixed and constant throughout life"},{"label":"B","text":"It responds to infection in a nonspecific manner"},{"label":"C","text":"It develops a targeted response for each invader encountered"},{"label":"D","text":"It relies on phagocytes alone"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"immunoglobulins-5","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Adaptive immunity generates antigen-specific responses and immunologic memory, unlike the fixed pattern-recognition mechanisms of innate immunity."},{"source_number":"6","prompt":"The basic IgG molecule consists of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"two light chains only"},{"label":"B","text":"two identical heavy chains and two identical light chains linked by disulfide bonds"},{"label":"C","text":"four identical heavy chains"},{"label":"D","text":"three heavy chains and one light chain"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"immunoglobulins-6","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"An IgG monomer has two identical heavy chains and two identical light chains joined by disulfide bonds."},{"source_number":"7","prompt":"Immunoglobulin chains were designated \"heavy\" and \"light\" on the basis of their:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"rate of migration during SDS-polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis"},{"label":"B","text":"isoelectric point"},{"label":"C","text":"carbohydrate content"},{"label":"D","text":"antigen specificity"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"immunoglobulins-7","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Heavy and light chains were distinguished by their different molecular sizes/migration during SDS-PAGE."},{"source_number":"8","prompt":"Which pair of heavy chains contains four constant (CH) domains rather than three?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gamma and alpha"},{"label":"B","text":"Alpha and delta"},{"label":"C","text":"Delta and gamma"},{"label":"D","text":"Mu and epsilon"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"immunoglobulins-8","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"IgM (mu) and IgE (epsilon) heavy chains have four constant domains; IgG, IgA, and IgD generally have three."},{"source_number":"9","prompt":"In IgG, the complement-binding site is located in which domain?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CH1"},{"label":"B","text":"CH2"},{"label":"C","text":"VH"},{"label":"D","text":"CL"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"immunoglobulins-9","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"The major complement-binding region of IgG is in the CH2 portion of the Fc region."},{"source_number":"10","prompt":"Papain and pepsin cleave IgG at the:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"hinge region between the CH1 and CH2 domains"},{"label":"B","text":"junction between VH and VL"},{"label":"C","text":"CH3 domain"},{"label":"D","text":"junction between CL and VL"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"immunoglobulins-10","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Papain and pepsin cleave around the hinge region between CH1 and CH2, producing different Fab/Fc-derived fragments."},{"source_number":"11","prompt":"The antigen-binding site of an immunoglobulin is formed by the:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CH2 and CH3 domains of both heavy chains"},{"label":"B","text":"constant regions of the light chains"},{"label":"C","text":"hypervariable regions of the VH and VL domains"},{"label":"D","text":"J chain"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"immunoglobulins-11","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Antigen specificity is created by the paired variable domains of heavy and light chains, especially their hypervariable CDRs."},{"source_number":"12","prompt":"Hypervariable regions, also called complementarity-determining regions (CDRs), are best described as:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"long stretches of more than 50 residues within the constant region"},{"label":"B","text":"sites that bind Fc receptors on phagocytes"},{"label":"C","text":"the sites that bind complement"},{"label":"D","text":"short islands of about 5 to 10 residues within the variable regions"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"immunoglobulins-12","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"CDRs are short hypervariable stretches within variable regions that directly form the antigen-binding surface."},{"source_number":"13","prompt":"In the alternative pathway of complement activation, C3 is activated by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"direct chemical hydrolysis (\"ticking over\")"},{"label":"B","text":"an antibody-antigen complex"},{"label":"C","text":"binding of mannose-binding lectin"},{"label":"D","text":"cleavage by activated C1"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"immunoglobulins-13","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"The alternative pathway begins with spontaneous low-level hydrolysis ('tickover') of C3 and subsequent amplification on suitable surfaces."},{"source_number":"14","prompt":"Once exposed by proteolytic activation, the thioester bonds of C3 and C4 react with:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"nucleic acids of the pathogen"},{"label":"B","text":"the Fab regions of antibodies"},{"label":"C","text":"hydroxyl groups on bacterial surface polysaccharides"},{"label":"D","text":"complement protein C9 only"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"immunoglobulins-14","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Activated C3/C4 thioesters can covalently bind hydroxyl groups on pathogen surface carbohydrates/proteins."},{"source_number":"15","prompt":"Which statement about immunoglobulin light chains is correct?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A single molecule usually contains one kappa and one lambda chain"},{"label":"B","text":"A given molecule contains either two kappa or two lambda chains, never one of each"},{"label":"C","text":"Lambda chains predominate in humans"},{"label":"D","text":"Kappa and lambda chains differ only in their variable regions"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"immunoglobulins-15","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Each immunoglobulin molecule contains either two kappa or two lambda light chains, not one of each."},{"source_number":"16","prompt":"The heavy chain of IgA is designated:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"alpha"},{"label":"B","text":"delta"},{"label":"C","text":"epsilon"},{"label":"D","text":"mu"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"immunoglobulins-16","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"The IgA heavy chain is the alpha chain."},{"source_number":"17","prompt":"Which of the following illustrates acute autoimmune destruction of a tissue?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rheumatoid arthritis"},{"label":"B","text":"Multiple sclerosis"},{"label":"C","text":"Agammaglobulinemia"},{"label":"D","text":"Type 1 diabetes mellitus"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"immunoglobulins-17","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Type 1 diabetes is an example of immune-mediated destruction of a specific tissue/cell population."},{"source_number":"18","prompt":"Approximately what percentage of total serum immunoglobulin is IgG?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"75%"},{"label":"B","text":"15%"},{"label":"C","text":"9%"},{"label":"D","text":"0.2%"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"immunoglobulins-18","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"IgG accounts for roughly three quarters of serum immunoglobulin."},{"source_number":"19","prompt":"Which immunoglobulin is the first class produced in a primary response and circulates as a pentamer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IgG"},{"label":"B","text":"IgA"},{"label":"C","text":"IgE"},{"label":"D","text":"IgM"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"immunoglobulins-19","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"IgM is produced first in a primary response and is secreted mainly as a pentamer."},{"source_number":"20","prompt":"Secretory IgA contains which additional components?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A J chain only"},{"label":"B","text":"A secretory component only"},{"label":"C","text":"Both a J chain and a secretory component"},{"label":"D","text":"Neither a J chain nor a secretory component"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"immunoglobulins-20","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Secretory IgA is typically a dimer linked by J chain and associated with a secretory component."},{"source_number":"21","prompt":"The principal protective action of secretory IgA is to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"fix complement"},{"label":"B","text":"prevent attachment of bacteria and viruses to mucous membranes"},{"label":"C","text":"cross the placenta"},{"label":"D","text":"trigger degranulation of mast cells"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"immunoglobulins-21","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Secretory IgA protects mucosa largely by neutralizing pathogens and preventing their attachment."},{"source_number":"22","prompt":"Which statement about IgE is correct?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It mediates immediate hypersensitivity by triggering mediator release from mast cells and basophils"},{"label":"B","text":"It fixes complement"},{"label":"C","text":"It crosses the placenta"},{"label":"D","text":"It is the most abundant immunoglobulin in serum"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"immunoglobulins-22","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"IgE bound to mast cells/basophils mediates immediate hypersensitivity when allergen cross-links the receptors."},{"source_number":"23","prompt":"Agammaglobulinemia is characterized by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"overproduction of autoantibodies"},{"label":"B","text":"markedly reduced production of IgG"},{"label":"C","text":"absence of complement protein C3"},{"label":"D","text":"overproduction of mannose-binding lectin"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"immunoglobulins-23","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Agammaglobulinemia is characterized by markedly reduced immunoglobulin production, prominently IgG."},{"source_number":"24","prompt":"Which immunoglobulin classes fix complement?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IgA and IgE"},{"label":"B","text":"IgD and IgE"},{"label":"C","text":"IgG and IgM"},{"label":"D","text":"IgA and IgD"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"immunoglobulins-24","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"IgM and many IgG subclasses can activate the classical complement pathway."},{"source_number":"25","prompt":"IgM is said to opsonize only indirectly because:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"complement activation by IgM generates C3b, which is an opsonin"},{"label":"B","text":"it binds Fc receptors on macrophages through its J chain"},{"label":"C","text":"its pentameric size prevents direct binding to bacteria"},{"label":"D","text":"it lacks a constant region"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"immunoglobulins-25","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"IgM promotes opsonization mainly by efficiently activating complement and generating surface-bound C3b."},{"source_number":"26","prompt":"The human genome contains fewer than 150 immunoglobulin genes, yet each person can synthesize perhaps how many different antibodies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,000"},{"label":"B","text":"10,000"},{"label":"C","text":"1 million"},{"label":"D","text":"1 billion"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"immunoglobulins-26","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Combinatorial V(D)J assembly, junctional diversity, heavy-light pairing, and somatic hypermutation permit an antibody repertoire on the order of billions."},{"source_number":"27","prompt":"Which gene segment contributes to immunoglobulin heavy chains but not to light chains?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Variable (V)"},{"label":"B","text":"Joining (J)"},{"label":"C","text":"Constant (C)"},{"label":"D","text":"Diversity (D)"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"immunoglobulins-27","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"D (diversity) segments are used in heavy-chain VDJ rearrangement but not light-chain VJ rearrangement."},{"source_number":"28","prompt":"Activation-induced cytidine deaminase (AID) increases antibody diversity by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"converting cytidine to uracil, greatly raising the mutation frequency of immunoglobulin V genes"},{"label":"B","text":"joining J segments to V segments"},{"label":"C","text":"switching light chains to heavy chains"},{"label":"D","text":"hydrolyzing C3"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"immunoglobulins-28","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"AID deaminates cytidine to uracil in immunoglobulin loci and drives somatic hypermutation/class-switch recombination."},{"source_number":"29","prompt":"Mutations generated by AID and by junctional diversity are described as:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"germline and inherited by offspring"},{"label":"B","text":"restricted to T cells"},{"label":"C","text":"somatic, unique to the differentiated cell in which they occurred"},{"label":"D","text":"mitochondrial"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"immunoglobulins-29","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Somatic hypermutation and junctional changes occur in lymphocyte lineages and are not inherited through the germline."},{"source_number":"30","prompt":"Junctional diversity refers to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"pairing of different heavy chains with the same light chain"},{"label":"B","text":"random addition or deletion of nucleotides where gene segments are joined"},{"label":"C","text":"mutation of V genes by AID"},{"label":"D","text":"disulfide bonding between chains"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"immunoglobulins-30","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Junctional diversity is created by imprecise joining plus nucleotide addition/deletion at V(D)J junctions."},{"source_number":"31","prompt":"Class (isotype) switching involves:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"a change in the epitope specificity of the antibody"},{"label":"B","text":"conversion of kappa light chains to lambda light chains"},{"label":"C","text":"replacement of the VH region by a new V gene"},{"label":"D","text":"pairing the same antigen-specific light chain with a different heavy chain, preserving specificity"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"immunoglobulins-31","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Class switching changes the heavy-chain constant region while preserving the existing antigen-binding variable region and specificity."},{"source_number":"32","prompt":"Immunoglobulin molecules with identical hypervariable and variable domains, and hence identical epitope specificity, are said to share a common:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"idiotype"},{"label":"B","text":"isotype"},{"label":"C","text":"allotype"},{"label":"D","text":"J chain"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"immunoglobulins-32","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Antibodies with the same unique variable-region antigen-binding determinants share an idiotype."},{"source_number":"33","prompt":"Monoclonal antibodies are best characterized as:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"a heterogeneous mixture of antibodies from many B-cell clones"},{"label":"B","text":"antibodies obtained directly from the serum of an immunized rabbit"},{"label":"C","text":"homogeneous antibodies that target a single epitope"},{"label":"D","text":"antibodies that include all of the host animal's immunoglobulins"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"immunoglobulins-33","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Monoclonal antibodies are homogeneous immunoglobulins derived from one clone and bind the same epitope."},{"source_number":"34","prompt":"A hybridoma is produced by fusing:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"two B cells"},{"label":"B","text":"antigen-primed B cells with myeloma cells"},{"label":"C","text":"T cells with B cells"},{"label":"D","text":"plasma cells with macrophages"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"immunoglobulins-34","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Hybridomas fuse antigen-primed B cells with immortal myeloma cells to combine antibody production with unlimited growth."},{"source_number":"35","prompt":"Humanization of a murine monoclonal antibody is accomplished by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"inserting the murine variable regions into a human immunoglobulin molecule"},{"label":"B","text":"inserting human constant regions into a mouse B cell"},{"label":"C","text":"fusing human B cells with human myeloma cells"},{"label":"D","text":"removing the J chain"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"immunoglobulins-35","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Among these choices, insertion of murine antigen-binding variable sequences into a largely human immunoglobulin is closest to antibody humanization; strictly, whole murine variable regions produce a chimeric antibody, whereas true humanization usually grafts murine CDRs.","key_note":"Terminology caveat: option A is the closest choice, but whole murine variable regions + human constant regions are more precisely called a chimeric antibody; humanized antibodies usually retain only murine CDRs."},{"source_number":"36","prompt":"The classical pathway of complement activation is initiated when:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"mannose-binding lectin binds mannans"},{"label":"B","text":"C3 is hydrolyzed spontaneously"},{"label":"C","text":"factor D cleaves factor B"},{"label":"D","text":"an antibody-antigen complex binds to and activates C1"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"immunoglobulins-36","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"review_flag"},{"source_number":"37","prompt":"The term lectin refers to any:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"enzyme that cleaves C4"},{"label":"B","text":"class of immunoglobulin"},{"label":"C","text":"protein that binds polysaccharides"},{"label":"D","text":"hormone that regulates complement"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"immunoglobulins-37","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"The classical complement pathway is initiated when antigen-bound antibody activates C1."},{"source_number":"38","prompt":"All three pathways of complement activation converge on cleavage of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C1"},{"label":"B","text":"C4"},{"label":"C","text":"factor B"},{"label":"D","text":"C3"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"immunoglobulins-38","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"A lectin is a protein that specifically binds carbohydrate structures."},{"source_number":"39","prompt":"The membrane attack complex is assembled when C5b combines with:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C6, C7, C8 and C9"},{"label":"B","text":"C1q, C1r and C1s"},{"label":"C","text":"C3a and C5a"},{"label":"D","text":"factors B and D"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"immunoglobulins-39","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"All complement pathways converge on formation of a C3 convertase and cleavage of C3."},{"source_number":"40","prompt":"In primary amyloidosis, the fibrils are derived principally from:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"serum amyloid A"},{"label":"B","text":"immunoglobulin light chains"},{"label":"C","text":"transthyretin"},{"label":"D","text":"beta-2 microglobulin"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"immunoglobulins-40","set_id":"immunoglobulins","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"C5b recruits C6, C7, C8, and polymerized C9 to form the membrane attack complex."}]},{"id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","title":"Doc Medith — Immune System Exam","subtitle":"Immune Physiology & Hypersensitivity","source":"immune-physiology.docx","key_status":"not_provided","questions":[{"source_number":"1","prompt":"Which of the following is a primary lymphoid tissue?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spleen"},{"label":"B","text":"Skin"},{"label":"C","text":"Thymus"},{"label":"D","text":"Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue"},{"label":"E","text":"Lymph node"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"immune-physio-1","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"The thymus is a primary lymphoid organ where T cells mature."},{"source_number":"2","prompt":"Which of the following is part of the first-line defense system in the human body, but not considered as part of the immune system?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neutrophils"},{"label":"B","text":"Plasma cells"},{"label":"C","text":"Commensal organisms"},{"label":"D","text":"Macrophages"},{"label":"E","text":"Natural killer cells"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"immune-physio-2","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Commensal microbes are a first-line protective barrier but are not themselves immune-system cells."},{"source_number":"3","prompt":"Initial exposure to mucosal pathogens for the generation of the adaptive immune response takes place in which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue"},{"label":"B","text":"Blood"},{"label":"C","text":"Thymus"},{"label":"D","text":"Bone marrow"},{"label":"E","text":"Spleen"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"immune-physio-3","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"MALT is positioned at mucosal surfaces where antigens are sampled and adaptive responses are initiated."},{"source_number":"3","prompt":"Which of the following is true regarding innate immune response and adaptive immune response?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Innate immune response mainly originates in the secondary lymphoid tissue."},{"label":"B","text":"Innate immune response is rather slow compared to the adaptive immune response."},{"label":"C","text":"Innate immune response is more specific than adaptive immune response."},{"label":"D","text":"Innate immune response occurs within hours."}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"immune-physio-4","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Innate responses begin within minutes to hours; adaptive responses are slower but more specific."},{"source_number":"6","prompt":"Which of the following are recognized by the pathogen recognition receptor in the innate immune system?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Only damage associated molecular patterns (DAMPs)"},{"label":"B","text":"Pathogen associated molecular patterns (PAMPs) and damage associated molecular patterns (DAMPs)"},{"label":"C","text":"Only pathogen associated molecular patterns (PAMPs) and specific antigens"},{"label":"D","text":"Only damage associated molecular patterns (DAMPs) and specific antigens"},{"label":"E","text":"Only specific antigens"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"immune-physio-5","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Pattern-recognition receptors detect conserved PAMPs and signals of cellular injury such as DAMPs."},{"source_number":"7","prompt":"What is the typical amount of time required for a primary adaptive immune response to reach peak levels?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6-12 hours"},{"label":"B","text":"2-3 weeks"},{"label":"C","text":"2-3 months"},{"label":"D","text":"2-10 days"},{"label":"E","text":"1-6 hours"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"immune-physio-6","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"A primary adaptive response usually reaches substantial/peak levels over several days, commonly about 2–10 days in this course framing."},{"source_number":"8","prompt":"Which of the following pathogens cannot be eliminated through phagocytosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Viruses"},{"label":"B","text":"Tapeworms"},{"label":"C","text":"Fungi"},{"label":"D","text":"Bacteria"},{"label":"E","text":"Plasmodia"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"immune-physio-7","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Large multicellular parasites such as tapeworms are too large to be engulfed by phagocytes and require extracellular immune mechanisms."},{"source_number":"9","prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a typical method of eliminating pathogens by the immune system?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pathogens are destroyed inside a phagocytic vacuole in an immune cell."},{"label":"B","text":"Immune cells release factors that activate macrophages."},{"label":"C","text":"Neutrophils and macrophages engulf pathogens."},{"label":"D","text":"Neutrophils enter the pathogen and lyse the pathogen from within."},{"label":"E","text":"Immune cells kill host cells that have been infected with pathogen."}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"immune-physio-8","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Neutrophils do not enter a pathogen and lyse it from within; they engulf microbes or release antimicrobial products extracellularly."},{"source_number":"10","prompt":"What structural organization allows immune cells access to all parts of the body?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Interstitial fluid"},{"label":"B","text":"Subcutaneous tissue"},{"label":"C","text":"Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue"},{"label":"D","text":"The dual blood and lymphatic circulation"},{"label":"E","text":"Bone marrow"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"immune-physio-9","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Blood and lymphatic circulations connect tissues and lymphoid organs, allowing immune-cell trafficking throughout the body."},{"source_number":"11","prompt":"Which of the following is a main type of interaction between cells of the immune system?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Binding of a ligand on one cell to the nucleus of another cell"},{"label":"B","text":"Binding of a ligand on one cell to the receptor on the other"},{"label":"C","text":"Phagocytosis"},{"label":"D","text":"Secreted molecules from one cell altering nuclear DNA of another cell"},{"label":"E","text":"Apoptosis"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"immune-physio-10","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Immune cells commonly communicate by cell-surface ligand–receptor interactions and by soluble mediators."},{"source_number":"12","prompt":"What is the initial trigger of an immune response to a virus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Response by the adaptive immune system"},{"label":"B","text":"Secretion of molecules from an immune cell that destroy the infected cell"},{"label":"C","text":"Secretion of molecules from an immune cell that recruit other immune cells"},{"label":"D","text":"Detection of the presence of a pathogen"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"immune-physio-11","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"The first event is recognition/detection of the pathogen or pathogen-associated signals."},{"source_number":"13","prompt":"Cessation of the immune response is usually mainly a result of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Damage-associated molecular patterns"},{"label":"B","text":"Elimination of the pathogen"},{"label":"C","text":"Regulatory cells that suppress immune response"},{"label":"D","text":"Release of Interleukin 10 and transforming growth factor beta"},{"label":"E","text":"Pathogen-associated molecular patterns"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"immune-physio-12","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Removal of the triggering pathogen removes the main stimulus sustaining the response; regulatory mechanisms then help terminate it."},{"source_number":"14","prompt":"Which of the following cells mainly develops from common lymphoid progenitors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Basophil"},{"label":"B","text":"Eosinophil"},{"label":"C","text":"Natural killer cell"},{"label":"D","text":"Monocyte"},{"label":"E","text":"Neutrophil"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"immune-physio-13","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"NK cells arise from the common lymphoid progenitor lineage."},{"source_number":"15","prompt":"Which phagocytic cells circulate to reach distant sites of infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Eosinophils"},{"label":"B","text":"Natural killer cells"},{"label":"C","text":"Neutrophils"},{"label":"D","text":"Basophils"},{"label":"E","text":"Macrophages"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"immune-physio-14","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Neutrophils circulate in blood and are rapidly recruited to distant sites of acute infection."},{"source_number":"16","prompt":"Which of the following components of a bacterium is cleaved by a lysozyme?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phospholipid"},{"label":"B","text":"Peptidoglycan"},{"label":"C","text":"Flagellum"},{"label":"D","text":"Ribosome"},{"label":"E","text":"Phosphatidylethanolamine"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"immune-physio-15","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Lysozyme cleaves bonds in bacterial peptidoglycan."},{"source_number":"17","prompt":"Which of the following cells is a phagocytic cell found ONLY in the extravascular compartment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Basophil"},{"label":"B","text":"Neutrophil"},{"label":"C","text":"Eosinophil"},{"label":"D","text":"Monocyte"},{"label":"E","text":"Macrophage"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"E","id":"immune-physio-16","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Macrophages are tissue-resident/extravascular phagocytes; circulating precursors are monocytes."},{"source_number":"18","prompt":"Fc receptors (FcR) on the surface of neutrophils, macrophages and other immune cells recognize which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antibodies that have coated antigens"},{"label":"B","text":"Macrophages that have encountered an antigen"},{"label":"C","text":"Antigens on pathogens"},{"label":"D","text":"Pathogen-associated molecular patterns"},{"label":"E","text":"Complement molecules on a pathogen"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"immune-physio-17","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Fc receptors recognize the Fc portion of antibodies coating antigens/pathogens and thereby promote opsonization-dependent effector functions."},{"source_number":"19","prompt":"If the production of lysosomes is impaired, which step of phagocytosis is compromised?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Invagination"},{"label":"B","text":"Complement recognition by the complement receptor"},{"label":"C","text":"Recognition of pathogens"},{"label":"D","text":"Enzymatic degradation of pathogens within the phagolysosome"},{"label":"E","text":"Effective binding to pathogen-associated molecular pattern"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"immune-physio-18","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Lysosomes supply hydrolytic enzymes needed to kill and degrade material after phagosome–lysosome fusion."},{"source_number":"20","prompt":"Which of the following is most characteristic of eosinophilic granules?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leukotrienes"},{"label":"B","text":"Major basic protein"},{"label":"C","text":"Interferon"},{"label":"D","text":"Prostaglandins"},{"label":"E","text":"Histamine"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"immune-physio-19","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Eosinophil specific granules contain major basic protein, important in helminth killing."},{"source_number":"21","prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a characteristic of a dendritic cell?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Derived from hematopoietic progenitor cells"},{"label":"B","text":"Present in tissues in contact with the external environment"},{"label":"C","text":"Possess MHC class II and costimulatory molecules"},{"label":"D","text":"Initially phagocytic"},{"label":"E","text":"Specialized to present antigens mainly to B cells"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"E","id":"immune-physio-20","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Dendritic cells are specialized to activate T cells, especially naïve T cells—not mainly B cells."},{"source_number":"22","prompt":"Which of the following cells is a part of the innate immune system and may induce apoptotic cell death via the FasR/FasL pathway?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Natural killer Cell"},{"label":"B","text":"Basophil"},{"label":"C","text":"Eosinophil"},{"label":"D","text":"Monocyte"},{"label":"E","text":"Mast cell"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"immune-physio-21","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"NK cells can trigger apoptosis through death-receptor pathways such as FasL/Fas as well as perforin/granzyme mechanisms."},{"source_number":"23","prompt":"Which of the following adaptive immunity cells function most similarly to natural killer cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Macrophage"},{"label":"B","text":"Cytotoxic T cell"},{"label":"C","text":"Mast cell"},{"label":"D","text":"Dendritic cell"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"immune-physio-22","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Cytotoxic T lymphocytes most closely resemble NK cells because both kill target cells by inducing apoptosis."},{"source_number":"24","prompt":"Which of the following best describes the role of proteases in antigen presentation to T cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proteases bind peptides to major histocompatibility complex molecules."},{"label":"B","text":"Proteases act as antigens for binding to the major histocompatibility complex molecules."},{"label":"C","text":"Proteases signal T cell receptors for binding to major histocompatibility complex molecules."},{"label":"D","text":"Proteases process proteins into peptides for binding to the major histocompatibility complex molecules."},{"label":"E","text":"Proteases strengthen the bond between the major histocompatibility complex molecule and the T cell receptor."}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"immune-physio-23","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Proteases generate peptide fragments that can bind MHC molecules for T-cell recognition."},{"source_number":"25","prompt":"What is an example of an MHC class II molecule?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HLA-C"},{"label":"B","text":"Factor B"},{"label":"C","text":"HLA-B"},{"label":"D","text":"HLA-DR"},{"label":"E","text":"HLA-A"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"immune-physio-24","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"HLA-DR is a classical MHC class II molecule."},{"source_number":"26","prompt":"Which of the following comparisons between MHC Class I and MHC Class II is CORRECT?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"MHC I: Alerts helper T cells MHC II: Alerts cytotoxic T cells"},{"label":"B","text":"MHC I: Present on all nucleated cells in the body MHC II: Present on antigen-presenting cells"},{"label":"C","text":"MHC I: Alerts CD4+ T cells MHC II: Alerts CD8+ T cells"},{"label":"D","text":"MHC I: Present on red blood cells MHC II: Present on antigen-presenting cells"},{"label":"E","text":"MHC I: Binds peptides derived from extracellular antigens MHC II: Binds peptides derived from intracellular antigens"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"immune-physio-25","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"MHC I is expressed on nearly all nucleated cells; MHC II is concentrated on professional antigen-presenting cells."},{"source_number":"27","prompt":"How many different classical MHC molecules are expressed in each individual?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"12-14"},{"label":"B","text":"2-6"},{"label":"C","text":"32-56"},{"label":"D","text":"100"},{"label":"E","text":"Several million"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"immune-physio-26","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"An individual can express multiple codominant classical HLA class I and II molecules; the offered 12–14 range is the best fit to this course item.","key_note":"Course-style item; wording/options are simplified. Use the rationale and compare with your instructor's framing if discussed differently."},{"source_number":"28","prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a receptor location of pattern recognition receptor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Membrane-bound"},{"label":"B","text":"Cell-surface"},{"label":"C","text":"Nuclear"},{"label":"D","text":"Cytoplasmic"},{"label":"E","text":"Secreted"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"immune-physio-27","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Pattern-recognition receptors may be surface/endosomal, cytoplasmic, or soluble; the nucleus is not a typical PRR compartment."},{"source_number":"29","prompt":"Which of the following is a major difference between B-cell receptors (BCR) and T-cell receptors (TCR)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Only TCRs can be found on the cell surface."},{"label":"B","text":"Only TCRs are highly specific."},{"label":"C","text":"Only TCRs are part of the adaptive immune system."},{"label":"D","text":"Only BCRs are highly specific."},{"label":"E","text":"Only B-cell receptors can be secreted."}],"type":"mcq","answer":"E","id":"immune-physio-28","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"BCR can exist in a secreted form as antibody, whereas TCR remains a membrane receptor."},{"source_number":"30","prompt":"What is the molecule that prevents the binding of endogenous peptides to the binding groove on the major histocompatibility complex (MHC) class II within the endoplasmic reticulum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Invariant chain (Ii)"},{"label":"B","text":"Erp57"},{"label":"C","text":"Transporter associated with antigen processing 1"},{"label":"D","text":"Tapasin"},{"label":"E","text":"Transporter associated with antigen processing 2"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"immune-physio-29","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Invariant chain occupies the MHC II peptide-binding groove in the ER and directs the complex to endosomal compartments."},{"source_number":"34","prompt":"Which of the following approximate peptide lengths are most appropriate for binding major histocompatibility complex (MHC) class I and II, respectively?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"8-9 amino acids and 15 amino acids"},{"label":"B","text":"80-90 amino acids and 150 amino acids"},{"label":"C","text":"The appropriate number of amino acids does not vary between the two MHC classes."},{"label":"D","text":"15 amino acids and 8-9 amino acids"},{"label":"E","text":"150 amino acids and 80-90 amino acids"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"immune-physio-30","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"MHC I typically binds short peptides of about 8–10 residues, while MHC II accommodates longer peptides, often around 13–18 residues."},{"source_number":"35","prompt":"Which of the following best defines antigen cross-presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Presentation of exogenous antigens through MHC class I molecules to cytotoxic T cells"},{"label":"B","text":"Presentation of exogenous antigens through MHC class II molecules to cytotoxic T cells"},{"label":"C","text":"Presentation of endogenous antigens through MHC class II molecules to cytotoxic B cells"},{"label":"D","text":"Presentation of exogenous antigens through MHC class I molecules to helper T cells"},{"label":"E","text":"Presentation of endogenous antigens through MHC class I molecules to helper T cells"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"immune-physio-31","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Cross-presentation is the presentation of exogenous antigen on MHC I to CD8+ cytotoxic T cells."},{"source_number":"36","prompt":"Which of the following options best compares primary immune response to secondary immune response?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Secondary is slower to respond than primary."},{"label":"B","text":"Antibody levels decline more rapidly in the secondary form."},{"label":"C","text":"Primary is of lower magnitude than secondary."},{"label":"D","text":"Unlike the secondary immune response, the primary form involves memory cells."},{"label":"E","text":"Secondary is less specific than primary."}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"immune-physio-32","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Secondary responses are faster and larger; therefore the primary response is lower in magnitude."},{"source_number":"37","prompt":"Which of the following best describes the process of clonal selection in immune response?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"How a single B or T cell specific to an antigen is selected and reproduced to generate a clonal cell population that eliminates the antigen"},{"label":"B","text":"How a single pathogen that infects an immune cell is presented to memory cells for eradication"},{"label":"C","text":"How a single infecting pathogen selects and proliferates within a specific clone of immune cells."},{"label":"D","text":"How the innate immune system mounts a response to different antigens in the same fashion."}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"immune-physio-33","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Clonal selection expands the rare B/T lymphocyte whose receptor specifically recognizes the antigen."},{"source_number":"38","prompt":"Which of the following cells differentiate from a naive B cell after exposure to a specific antigen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cytotoxic T cells and natural killer cells"},{"label":"B","text":"Plasma cells and natural killer cells"},{"label":"C","text":"Plasma cells and memory B cells"},{"label":"D","text":"Memory B cells and natural killer cells"},{"label":"E","text":"Cytotoxic T cells and plasma cells"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"immune-physio-34","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Activated naïve B cells differentiate into antibody-secreting plasma cells and long-lived memory B cells."},{"source_number":"39","prompt":"Which of the following best describes the key role of memory B cells in the human adaptive immune response?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They produce antigen-specific antibodies more efficiently than other types of cells."},{"label":"B","text":"They recruit T cells to help respond to antigens."},{"label":"C","text":"They are responsible for mounting the secondary immune response."},{"label":"D","text":"They initiate and maintain a primary immune response."},{"label":"E","text":"They secrete cytokines which cause naive B cells to differentiate into a specific antigen target."}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"immune-physio-35","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Memory B cells are central to the rapid, amplified secondary humoral response."},{"source_number":"40","prompt":"What is the first barrier that must be overcome by microbes to cause infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The complement system"},{"label":"B","text":"Epithelial cells"},{"label":"C","text":"B lymphocytes"},{"label":"D","text":"Natural killer cells"},{"label":"E","text":"T lymphocytes"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"immune-physio-36","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Intact epithelium is the first physical barrier that microbes must breach to establish infection."},{"source_number":"41","prompt":"What cells are involved in the adaptive immune response?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neutrophils"},{"label":"B","text":"T lymphocytes and B lymphocytes"},{"label":"C","text":"Mast cells"},{"label":"D","text":"Epithelial cells"},{"label":"E","text":"Natural killer cells"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"immune-physio-37","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"The adaptive immune system is built primarily around antigen-specific B and T lymphocytes."},{"source_number":"42","prompt":"Which cytokine is involved in macrophage activation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IL-2"},{"label":"B","text":"IL-5"},{"label":"C","text":"Interferon-gamma"},{"label":"D","text":"IL-4"},{"label":"E","text":"IL-3"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"immune-physio-38","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"IFN-gamma is a major macrophage-activating cytokine, especially from Th1 and NK cells."},{"source_number":"43","prompt":"What is the function of IL-4?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is involved in cytotoxic lymphocyte activation."},{"label":"B","text":"It is involved in the clonal expansion of helper T lymphocytes."},{"label":"C","text":"It is involved in natural killer cell maturation."},{"label":"D","text":"It is involved in B lymphocyte activation."},{"label":"E","text":"It is involved in macrophage activation."}],"type":"mcq","answer":"D","id":"immune-physio-39","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"IL-4 promotes B-cell activation and supports Th2 responses and class switching."},{"source_number":"44","prompt":"What type of cells are mainly involved in type IV hypersensitivity reactions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mast cells"},{"label":"B","text":"T cells"},{"label":"C","text":"B lymphocytes"},{"label":"D","text":"Macrophages"},{"label":"E","text":"Alpha cells"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"immune-physio-40","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Type IV (delayed) hypersensitivity is T-cell mediated."},{"source_number":"45","prompt":"What causes the immediate effects of IgE-mediated hypersensitivity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Allergens cross-linking IgE receptors on mast cells"},{"label":"B","text":"PAF produced by membrane phospholipids"},{"label":"C","text":"Phospholipase A2 breaking down histamine"},{"label":"D","text":"Mast cell degranulation releasing arachidonic acid"},{"label":"E","text":"Cytokines produced by mast cells"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"immune-physio-41","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Immediate IgE hypersensitivity begins when allergen cross-links IgE–FcεRI complexes on mast cells, triggering degranulation."},{"source_number":"46","prompt":"What is the most severe manifestation of IgE-mediated hypersensitivity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Asthma"},{"label":"B","text":"Seasonal rhinitis"},{"label":"C","text":"Food allergies"},{"label":"D","text":"Urticaria"},{"label":"E","text":"Anaphylaxis"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"E","id":"immune-physio-42","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Anaphylaxis is the most severe systemic manifestation of immediate IgE-mediated hypersensitivity."},{"source_number":"47","prompt":"What is involved in type III non-IgE-mediated reactions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They involve cell-mediated hypersensitivity reactions."},{"label":"B","text":"They involve immune complex deposition and complement/phagocyte activation."},{"label":"C","text":"They involve stimulating receptor-ligand interactions."},{"label":"D","text":"They involve blocking receptor-ligand interactions."},{"label":"E","text":"They involve cross-linking of antibodies on mast cells."}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"immune-physio-43","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Type III hypersensitivity is caused by immune-complex deposition followed by complement and phagocyte-driven inflammation."},{"source_number":"48","prompt":"Which of the following mechanisms is involved in the pathogenesis of type II hypersensitivity reactions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deposition of immune complexes in tissues and small blood vessels"},{"label":"B","text":"Cell-mediated cytotoxicity"},{"label":"C","text":"Autophagy"},{"label":"D","text":"Release of vasoactive amines and cytokines from mast cells"},{"label":"E","text":"Antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"E","id":"immune-physio-44","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity is one mechanism of type II antibody-mediated injury."},{"source_number":"49","prompt":"Which of the following diseases is an example of a type II hypersensitivity reaction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Contact dermatitis"},{"label":"B","text":"Rheumatoid arthritis"},{"label":"C","text":"Asthma"},{"label":"D","text":"Polyarteritis nodosa"},{"label":"E","text":"Autoimmune hemolytic anemia"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"E","id":"immune-physio-45","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Autoimmune hemolytic anemia is a classic type II hypersensitivity disorder in which antibodies target cell-surface antigens."},{"source_number":"50","prompt":"Which of the following complement subtypes is expressed on the surface of the target cells during a type II hypersensitivity reaction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C6"},{"label":"B","text":"C3a"},{"label":"C","text":"C5b"},{"label":"D","text":"C7"},{"label":"E","text":"C3b"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"E","id":"immune-physio-46","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"C3b becomes covalently deposited on target surfaces and acts as a major opsonin in antibody/complement-mediated injury."},{"source_number":"51","prompt":"Which of the following mechanisms is not involved in the pathogenesis of type II hypersensitivity reactions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Complement activation"},{"label":"B","text":"Opsonization"},{"label":"C","text":"T-helper-1 cell-mediated cytokine release"},{"label":"D","text":"Neutrophil and macrophage recruitment"},{"label":"E","text":"NK cell-mediated lysis"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"C","id":"immune-physio-47","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Th1-cell cytokine-mediated inflammation is a type IV mechanism, not a principal type II mechanism."},{"source_number":"52","prompt":"In a type III hypersensitivity reaction, where are immune complexes typically deposited?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epithelium"},{"label":"B","text":"Basement membrane"},{"label":"C","text":"Mesothelium"},{"label":"D","text":"Endothelium"},{"label":"E","text":"Lamina propria"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"B","id":"immune-physio-48","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Type III immune complexes commonly lodge in vessel walls and basement-membrane-rich filtration sites such as glomeruli; among the choices, basement membrane is the best match.","key_note":"Course-style item; wording/options are simplified. Use the rationale and compare with your instructor's framing if discussed differently."},{"source_number":"53","prompt":"A type IV hypersensitivity reaction is primarily mediated by which of the following cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T cells"},{"label":"B","text":"Eosinophils"},{"label":"C","text":"B cells"},{"label":"D","text":"NK cells"},{"label":"E","text":"Neutrophils"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"immune-physio-49","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Type IV hypersensitivity is mediated by sensitized T cells rather than antibodies."},{"source_number":"54","prompt":"Which of the following is an example of a type IV hypersensitivity reaction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myocarditis"},{"label":"B","text":"Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis"},{"label":"C","text":"Goodpasture disease"},{"label":"D","text":"Myasthenia gravis"},{"label":"E","text":"Autoimmune hemolytic anemia"}],"type":"mcq","answer":"A","id":"immune-physio-50","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"Among the choices, myocarditis is the best T-cell-mediated/type IV example; the other listed diseases are classic type II or III mechanisms.","key_note":"Course-style item; wording/options are simplified. Use the rationale and compare with your instructor's framing if discussed differently."},{"source_number":"55-60","prompt":"Illustrate the activation of the Complement cascade, including its pathways and its by-products (6 points).","options":[],"type":"essay","answer":null,"id":"immune-physio-51","set_id":"immune-physio","system":"Immune","key_status":"review","rationale":"For the complement cascade: classical (antibody/C1), lectin (MBL/MASP), and alternative pathways generate C3 convertases, cleave C3 to C3a/C3b, then form C5 convertases. C5a is a potent anaphylatoxin/chemoattractant, C3b opsonizes, and C5b-9 forms the membrane attack complex.","model_answer":"Classical pathway: antigen–antibody complexes activate C1 → C4/C2 → C3 convertase (C4b2a).\nLectin pathway: MBL/MASP binds microbial carbohydrates → C4/C2 → C4b2a.\nAlternative pathway: C3 tickover/surface amplification with factor B/D → C3bBb.\nAll cleave C3 → C3a + C3b; C3b forms C5 convertase → C5a + C5b; C5b recruits C6–C9 to form MAC.\nMajor by-products: C3b = opsonin; C3a/C5a = anaphylatoxins; C5a = strong chemoattractant; C5b-9 = membrane lysis."}]}]};